// ============================================================
//  LUMINA SANCTI — Biografias em inglês
// ============================================================
// Carregado só quando a pessoa escolhe English no site.
// Santo que ainda não está aqui aparece em português, com aviso.

window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS = window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS || {};
window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en = Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en || {}, {
  'sagrada-familia': {
    nome: `The Holy Family`,
    resumo: `Jesus, Mary and Joseph, the supreme model of love, faith and unity for every Christian family.`,
    texto: `<p>The Holy Family is the name Christian tradition gives to the family formed by Jesus, Mary and Joseph, held up through the centuries as a model of love, faith and unity for Christian families. Their story begins with the Annunciation: according to the Gospel of Luke, the angel Gabriel announces to Mary, a young woman of Nazareth still betrothed to Joseph, that she would conceive a son by the power of the Holy Spirit, and that this son would be called Jesus.</p>
<p>When Joseph learns that his betrothed is expecting a child, he goes through a moment of anguish and doubt, until an angel reassures him in a dream. From then on he faithfully protects the family, leading the flight into Egypt to escape Herod and later settling in Nazareth, where they live a simple life of work and prayer.</p>`,
  },
  'miguel': {
    nome: `Saint Michael the Archangel`,
    resumo: `The prince of the heavenly host and protector of the Church against the attacks of evil.`,
    texto: `<p>According to the Book of Revelation, it was Michael who led the faithful angels in the heavenly battle against Lucifer and the rebel angels, casting them out of Heaven. His name, which in Hebrew means "Who is like God?", is also his battle cry against the pride of the one who wanted to make himself equal to the Creator.</p>
<p>Throughout history, Michael has appeared at many moments as a defender of God's people, from the Old Testament to more recent Marian apparitions. He is invoked as a protector against evil in all its forms, and an ancient prayer composed by Pope Leo XIII asks for his daily protection against the snares of the devil.</p>`,
  },
  'gabriel': {
    nome: `Saint Gabriel the Archangel`,
    resumo: `God's messenger, who announced to Mary the birth of the Savior.`,
    texto: `<p>Known as the great herald of divine revelations, Saint Gabriel brought good news to Zechariah, announcing the birth of John the Baptist, and then the greatest message of all: the Annunciation to the Virgin Mary, revealing that she would be the mother of the Savior.</p>
<p>His name means "strength of God" or "God is my strength". Some biblical scholars also identify him with the angel who explained prophetic visions to the prophet Daniel centuries earlier. He is the patron of messengers, journalists, broadcasters and everyone who works in communications, for having carried the most important message ever announced.</p>`,
  },
  'rafael': {
    nome: `Saint Raphael the Archangel`,
    resumo: `The angel of healing and safe guide of travelers, as told in the Book of Tobit.`,
    texto: `<p>His name means "God heals". In the Book of Tobit, he appears disguised as a traveling companion to the young Tobias, guiding him safely through a dangerous journey and teaching him how to cure the blindness of his father, Tobit, with the gall of a fish.</p>
<p>Only at the end of the story does Raphael reveal his true angelic identity, saying that he presents the prayers of the saints before the glory of God. Because of this story, he is invoked as the protector of travelers, doctors, nurses and all who seek physical or spiritual healing.</p>`,
  },
  'agostinho': {
    nome: `Saint Augustine of Hippo`,
    resumo: `One of the greatest thinkers of the Church, who found the truth after a restless youth.`,
    texto: `<p>After a life of excess and endless philosophical searching, Augustine was converted through the tearful prayers of his mother, Saint Monica. He became Bishop of Hippo and one of the most influential theologians in history, famous for his "Confessions".</p>
<p>Before his conversion, he spent years given over to pleasure and to philosophical sects such as Manichaeism, always restlessly seeking the truth, a search he summed up in the famous words: "You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you." As a bishop he wrote works that shaped all of Western Christian thought, such as "The City of God", and he is considered one of the greatest theologians of all time.</p>`,
  },
  'tomas-aquino': {
    nome: `Saint Thomas Aquinas`,
    resumo: `The Angelic Doctor, whose wisdom brilliantly united faith and reason.`,
    texto: `<p>A Dominican friar of unmatched intellect, Thomas Aquinas devoted his life to explaining and defending the Christian faith through philosophy. His masterpiece, the "Summa Theologiae", is still a pillar of Christian study today.</p>
<p>Born into a noble Italian family, he scandalized his relatives by choosing to join the Dominicans, a mendicant order, instead of the prestigious church career planned for him; his brothers even held him captive for almost a year trying to change his mind. Although he was nicknamed the "dumb ox" in his youth because of his quietness, his teacher Albert the Great prophesied that his bellowing would one day echo throughout the whole world, which came true through his vast body of work, still studied in seminaries today.</p>`,
  },
  'teresa-avila': {
    nome: `Saint Teresa of Ávila`,
    resumo: `Mystic and reformer of Carmel, the first woman declared a Doctor of the Church.`,
    texto: `<p>A woman of strong character and a life of deep mental prayer, Saint Teresa reformed the Carmelite Order in the face of immense difficulties. She wrote great spiritual treatises, such as "The Interior Castle".</p>
<p>She entered Carmel while still young, but only after years of a lukewarm religious life did she experience a deeper conversion, which launched her into an intense mystical life marked by ecstasies and visions. She traveled across Spain, often ill and in harsh conditions, founding seventeen reformed convents, always with good humor; she used to say that "the Lord walks among the pots and pans too". In 1970 she was declared a Doctor of the Church, the first woman to receive that title.</p>`,
  },
  'antonio-padua': {
    nome: `Saint Anthony of Padua`,
    resumo: `The saint of miracles, great Franciscan preacher and Doctor of the Church.`,
    texto: `<p>Born in Lisbon, he was a brilliant theologian. He joined the Franciscan Order, and his preaching was so powerful that it drew huge crowds. He is the patron saint of the poor and is known as the "Ark of the Testament".</p>
<p>Before becoming a Franciscan, he was an Augustinian canon devoted to study; he changed orders after seeing the bodies of Franciscan friars martyred in Morocco, longing for missionary martyrdom himself. That never happened, but his life became a silent martyrdom of tireless preaching. He became known for preaching even to the fish when heretics refused to listen to him, and for his devotion to the Child Jesus, with whom he is often depicted in his arms.</p>`,
  },
  'francisco-sales': {
    nome: `Saint Francis de Sales`,
    resumo: `The gentle bishop, patron of journalists and writer of deep spirituality.`,
    texto: `<p>Known for his endless patience and gentle character, Francis de Sales converted thousands of Calvinists in the region of Geneva through love and explanatory pamphlets, which made him the patron of journalists. He is the author of the famous "Introduction to the Devout Life".</p>
<p>Born into a noble French family, he gave up a brilliant career in law and politics to become a priest, against his father's plans. As Bishop of Geneva, he insisted that holiness was not a privilege of religious people but a path open to lay people in any profession, a revolutionary idea in his time that made him one of the great spiritual masters of ordinary life.</p>`,
  },
  'catarina-sena': {
    nome: `Saint Catherine of Siena`,
    resumo: `A lay mystic whose courage convinced a Pope to return to Rome.`,
    texto: `<p>The daughter of a dyer from Siena, Catherine consecrated herself to God at a young age and lived a life of intense prayer and penance, without entering a convent, as a Dominican tertiary. She received the stigmata invisibly and devoted herself to caring for plague victims and prisoners condemned to death.</p>
<p>Her spiritual courage left its mark on history when, through letters and journeys, she convinced Pope Gregory XI to end the papacy's exile in Avignon and return to Rome. She left as her legacy "The Dialogue of Divine Providence" and, together with Saint Teresa of Ávila, was one of the first women declared Doctors of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'jeronimo': {
    nome: `Saint Jerome`,
    resumo: `The learned translator of the Bible into Latin, who lived as a hermit in the desert.`,
    texto: `<p>A scholar with a strong and restless temperament, Jerome devoted decades of his life to studying the Scriptures, even living as a hermit in the Syrian desert to deepen his prayer and penance. He learned Hebrew and Greek with a rigor unusual for his time.</p>
<p>His greatest legacy is the Vulgate, the translation of the entire Bible into Latin from the original texts, which became the official version used by the Church for more than a thousand years. He is considered the patron of translators, librarians and scholars of the Word of God.</p>`,
  },
  'ambrosio': {
    nome: `Saint Ambrose of Milan`,
    resumo: `Bishop of Milan, the great orator who baptized Saint Augustine.`,
    texto: `<p>A Roman governor who was still a catechumen when the people acclaimed him Bishop of Milan, Ambrose had to learn theology in a hurry to take on the mission entrusted to him. He became one of the greatest preachers of his time, firmly defending the faith even before emperors.</p>
<p>It was he who, through his eloquence and the example of his life, led the young rhetoric teacher Augustine to conversion, baptizing him on Easter night in 387. He also composed beautiful liturgical hymns that still resound in the Church's musical tradition today.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-crisostomo': {
    nome: `Saint John Chrysostom`,
    resumo: `"Golden Mouth", the greatest preacher of the early Church.`,
    texto: `<p>Patriarch of Constantinople, John was nicknamed "Chrysostom", Golden Mouth, for his extraordinary gift for preaching, combining theological depth with simple language that touched people's hearts. He fearlessly denounced the excesses of the powerful and their indifference toward the poor.</p>
<p>That courage cost him dearly: he was exiled twice by order of Empress Eudoxia, who felt targeted by his homilies. He died on the way to his second exile, leaving a vast legacy of biblical commentaries and sermons that remain among the most widely read of Christian antiquity.</p>`,
  },
  'gregorio-magno': {
    nome: `Saint Gregory the Great`,
    resumo: `The Pope who called himself "servant of the servants of God" and reformed the liturgy.`,
    texto: `<p>Born into a noble Roman family, Gregory left a promising political career to become a monk, but he was called back to public life and elected Pope in 590, amid famine, plague and barbarian invasions in Rome. He coined for himself the title "servant of the servants of God", which popes still use today.</p>
<p>He reorganized liturgical chant, now called Gregorian chant in his honor, and sent missionary monks to evangelize England. His wise leadership and his vast written work earned him the title "the Great".</p>`,
  },
  'boaventura': {
    nome: `Saint Bonaventure`,
    resumo: `The Seraphic Doctor, who guided the Franciscan Order with wisdom and love.`,
    texto: `<p>According to tradition, he was healed as a child by Saint Francis of Assisi, who, seeing him well again, is said to have exclaimed "O buona ventura!", what good fortune!, the origin of his religious name. He joined the Franciscans and became one of the greatest mystical theologians of his time, a close friend of Thomas Aquinas.</p>
<p>As Minister General of the Order, he wisely balanced the rigor of Franciscan spiritual life with the demands of a growing community. He wrote profound mystical works, such as "The Journey of the Mind to God", and received the title of Seraphic Doctor for the gentleness and fervor of his teaching.</p>`,
  },
  'alberto-magno': {
    nome: `Saint Albert the Great`,
    resumo: `The Universal Doctor, teacher of Thomas Aquinas and a pioneer in the study of nature.`,
    texto: `<p>A Dominican friar of insatiable curiosity, Albert devoted himself to studying almost every branch of knowledge of his time: philosophy, theology, botany, astronomy and mineralogy, which earned him the title of Universal Doctor. He believed that studying creation was also a way of knowing the Creator.</p>
<p>He taught in Paris and Cologne, where one of his students was a young man named Thomas Aquinas, whose genius he recognized and defended when others underestimated him. Because of his scientific pioneering within the faith, he is considered the patron saint of scientists.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-da-cruz': {
    nome: `Saint John of the Cross`,
    resumo: `Mystic poet and reformer of Carmel alongside Saint Teresa of Ávila.`,
    texto: `<p>A Carmelite friar small in stature and immense in soul, John joined Saint Teresa of Ávila in the reform of the Carmelite Order, which brought him persecution from his own religious brothers: he was even held prisoner and mistreated for months in a tiny cell in Toledo.</p>
<p>It was precisely in that captivity that he composed some of his most beautiful verses. His mystical poetry, "The Dark Night", "The Spiritual Canticle" and "The Living Flame of Love", describes with incomparable delicacy the soul's path toward union with God, and it continues to influence poets and mystics of every tradition.</p>`,
  },
  'hildegarda': {
    nome: `Saint Hildegard of Bingen`,
    resumo: `Visionary abbess, composer and student of nature in medieval Germany.`,
    texto: `<p>Given to a Benedictine monastery in Germany as a little girl, Hildegard said she had divine visions from childhood, which she began to write down only as an adult, with the Church's blessing. She became an abbess and one of the most respected spiritual voices of her time, advising popes and emperors.</p>
<p>She was also a composer, whose sacred melodies are still sung today, and she wrote detailed treatises on medicinal plants and the human body, a remarkable achievement for a woman of the twelfth century. She was declared a Doctor of the Church in 2012.</p>`,
  },
  'afonso-ligorio': {
    nome: `Saint Alphonsus Liguori`,
    resumo: `A lawyer who became a bishop and founder of the Redemptorists, master of moral theology.`,
    texto: `<p>A brilliant lawyer from Naples, Alphonsus left the legal profession after losing a case over a detail he had overlooked, understanding the episode as a call from God. He was ordained a priest devoted above all to evangelizing the poorest and most abandoned people of the countryside.</p>
<p>He founded the Congregation of the Most Holy Redeemer, the Redemptorists, and wrote a vast body of work on moral theology, always seeking to balance rigor with mercy in guiding souls. He is considered the patron of confessors and moral theologians.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-henry-newman': {
    nome: `Saint John Henry Newman`,
    resumo: `English cardinal who converted from Anglicanism, recently proclaimed a Doctor of the Church.`,
    texto: `<p>One of the greatest English intellectuals of the nineteenth century, Newman was a respected Anglican clergyman and professor at Oxford when, after years of study and a sincere search for the truth, he became a Catholic in 1845, a step that cost him friendships and prestige, but which he described as finally entering "a safe harbor".</p>
<p>Ordained a Catholic priest and later made a cardinal, he founded the Oratory in England and wrote fundamental works on conscience and on the development of Christian doctrine. Canonized in 2019, he was proclaimed a Doctor of the Church by Pope Leo XIV in November 2025, becoming co-patron of all those involved in education, together with Saint Thomas Aquinas.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-paulo-ii': {
    nome: `Saint John Paul II`,
    resumo: `The pilgrim Pope who traveled the world bringing the message of Christ to young people and families.`,
    texto: `<p>Karol Wojtyła, born in Poland, guided the Catholic Church through one of the longest and most remarkable pontificates in history. The founder of World Youth Day, he was a tireless defender of life, peace and human love.</p>
<p>He spent his youth under the Nazi occupation of Poland, working in a quarry and a chemical factory to avoid deportation, and later lived under the communist regime, secretly training for the priesthood. As pope, he survived an assassination attempt in 1981 and, years later, personally visited in prison the man who had tried to kill him, and forgave him. He traveled more than any previous pope, bringing the Christian message to more than a hundred countries, and was canonized in 2014.</p>`,
  },
  'francisco-assis': {
    nome: `Saint Francis of Assisi`,
    resumo: `The Poverello of Assisi, who gave up wealth to wed Lady Poverty and love all creation.`,
    texto: `<p>Born in Assisi, he gave up a life of parties and riches after a profound conversion, embracing a leper. He founded the Franciscan Order and saw God in all creation, composing the Canticle of the Creatures and receiving the stigmata before his death in 1226.</p>
<p>The son of a wealthy cloth merchant, he lived for the amusements of youth until illness and the experience of war led him to rethink his life; the decisive moment came before a crucifix in the chapel of San Damiano, which he heard say to him: "Francis, rebuild my Church." At first he took it literally, repairing chapels with his own hands, until he realized the mission was greater. He publicly renounced his father's inheritance, stripping off his own clothes in the town square, and began to live in the most radical poverty, attracting companions and founding one of the largest religious families in the history of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'jose': {
    nome: `Saint Joseph`,
    resumo: `The foster father of Jesus, a just man, patron of the Universal Church and model of workers.`,
    texto: `<p>Silent and deeply just, he welcomed Mary and protected the Child Jesus, working with his hands in Nazareth to provide for the Holy Family. He is the perfect example of fatherhood, care and obedience to God's will.</p>
<p>The Gospels do not record a single word of his: Joseph is the saint of silence and action, not of speeches. An angel instructs him in dreams three times: not to be afraid to take Mary as his wife, to flee with the family to Egypt because of Herod's threat, and later to return to Nazareth. A carpenter by trade, he taught his own work to Jesus. The Church proclaims him patron of the Universal Church, of workers and of a happy death, since it is believed that he died in the arms of Jesus and Mary.</p>`,
  },
  'padre-pio': {
    nome: `Saint Padre Pio of Pietrelcina`,
    resumo: `Capuchin friar who bore the stigmata of Christ and devoted his life to confession and prayer.`,
    texto: `<p>Francesco Forgione was born in 1887 in Italy. He joined the Capuchins, taking the name Pio. He was a man of prayer and deep suffering, who visibly received the stigmata in 1918. He spent endless hours in the confessional reconciling sinners with God.</p>
<p>He would spend up to sixteen hours a day hearing confessions, and accounts from his time say that he could perceive the hidden sins of penitents even before they confessed them, leading many to a deep conversion just by meeting him. Despite his fame and the crowds who sought him out, he lived for decades under suspicion and restrictions from the Church itself, which investigated the mystical phenomena around him for years; he always obeyed, even when it meant suffering. He also founded the "House for the Relief of Suffering", a large hospital for the poor.</p>`,
  },
  'faustina': {
    nome: `Saint Faustina Kowalska`,
    resumo: `The apostle of Divine Mercy, who received visions of the merciful Jesus.`,
    texto: `<p>A Polish religious sister with a monumental mystical life. Jesus appeared to her and entrusted her with the mission of spreading devotion to Divine Mercy throughout the world, which gave rise to the image of the Merciful Jesus and the Chaplet of Divine Mercy.</p>
<p>The daughter of poor peasants, she had little formal education and worked as a housemaid before she was able to enter religious life, being accepted as a lay sister in the Congregation of the Sisters of Our Lady of Mercy. At her confessor's request she recorded her mystical experiences in a diary, now published as "Diary: Divine Mercy in My Soul". It was through her that Pope John Paul II, her fellow Pole, established the Feast of Divine Mercy on the Sunday after Easter.</p>`,
  },
});

// Lote 2 (mais 25 santos)
Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en, {
  'dulce-pobres': {
    nome: `Saint Dulce of the Poor`,
    resumo: `The "Good Angel of Bahia", the first woman born in Brazil to be canonized.`,
    texto: `<p>Maria Rita devoted her whole life to caring for the sick and the destitute. She began by sheltering the sick in the henhouse of her convent, which would later become the largest charitable hospital in Brazil (the Irmã Dulce Social Works).</p>
<p>She died on March 13, 1992, in the middle of Lent — which is why the Archdiocese of Salvador, in agreement with the Vatican, set her liturgical feast on August 13, the day on which, still young, she made her religious vows and received the name Sister Dulce.</p>`,
  },
  'teresa-calcuta': {
    nome: `Saint Teresa of Calcutta`,
    resumo: `The "Mother of the Poor", who served the most abandoned on the streets of Calcutta.`,
    texto: `<p>Born Anjezë Gonxhe Bojaxhiu in 1910 in Skopje, she felt the call to religious life while still young and left for India, where for years she taught at a girls' school in Calcutta. It was there, in 1946, that she heard what she called "a call within a call": to leave everything to serve the poorest of the poor, on the streets.</p>
<p>She founded the Missionaries of Charity, a congregation that today works in more than a hundred countries, taking in the sick, the dying and the abandoned whom no one else would care for. She received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979 and gave the entire sum to the poor. Canonized in 2016, her feast was set on September 5, the day of her death in 1997.</p>`,
  },
  'bernadete': {
    nome: `Saint Bernadette Soubirous`,
    resumo: `The young visionary of Lourdes, who humbly welcomed the apparitions of Our Lady.`,
    texto: `<p>A humble, illiterate peasant girl from Lourdes, in France, to whom the Virgin Mary appeared 18 times at the Grotto of Massabielle. Despite all the interrogations and suspicion, Bernadette remained firm, serene and humble, and later lived as a nun with the Sisters of Nevers.</p>
<p>Suffering from asthma since childhood and living in extreme poverty, Bernadette could not even read when the first apparition took place, in 1858, which made her testimony all the more remarkable before skeptical authorities who questioned her again and again. During one of the apparitions the Lady asked her to dig in the ground — and there appeared the spring of water that to this day draws millions of pilgrims seeking healing. Only later did Bernadette reveal that the vision had identified herself by saying "I am the Immaculate Conception".</p>`,
  },
  'joana-darc': {
    nome: `Saint Joan of Arc`,
    resumo: `The Maid of Orléans, national heroine and warrior guided by heavenly voices.`,
    texto: `<p>A French peasant girl who, guided by the voices of Saint Michael, Saint Catherine and Saint Margaret, led the French army against English domination during the Hundred Years' War. She was captured, unjustly tried by a political court and burned alive at the stake at the age of 19.</p>
<p>She began hearing the heavenly voices at thirteen, and at seventeen she convinced the heir to the French throne, Charles VII, to entrust her with troops to free the besieged city of Orléans — a victory that changed the course of the war. Captured by the Burgundians and sold to the English, she was condemned for heresy, mainly for wearing men's clothing in battle. Twenty-five years after her death, a new trial fully cleared her name; she was canonized in 1920 and is today the patron saint of France.</p>`,
  },
  'lourenco': {
    nome: `Saint Lawrence`,
    resumo: `Roman deacon and martyr, famous for his good humor in the face of martyrdom.`,
    texto: `<p>A deacon of the Church in Rome, in charge of the diocese's goods and of helping the poor. When ordered to hand over the treasures of the Church to the Roman emperor, Lawrence presented the poor, the orphans and the sick, saying that they were the true treasures. He was martyred by being burned on a gridiron.</p>
<p>Faced with the fury of the Roman prefect at this answer, Lawrence was condemned to a slow martyrdom on an iron grill over hot coals. Tradition says that, in the midst of the torture, he still had the spirit to tell his executioners "you can turn me over, this side is done" — an episode that, over time, also made him the patron of cooks. His courage in the face of death impressed Rome so much that, within a few decades, his cult already rivaled that of the apostles Peter and Paul themselves.</p>`,
  },
  'inacio-loyola': {
    nome: `Saint Ignatius of Loyola`,
    resumo: `The Spanish soldier wounded in battle who became the founder of the Jesuits.`,
    texto: `<p>A vain and ambitious Spanish knight, Ignatius had his leg shattered by a cannonball during the defense of Pamplona. During his long and painful convalescence, with nothing else to read, he immersed himself in books about the life of Christ and the saints — and the course of his life changed completely.</p>
<p>He founded the Society of Jesus, an order marked by obedience, spiritual discipline and mission. His "Spiritual Exercises", born of his own conversion, remain one of the most widely practiced paths of prayer throughout the world to this day.</p>`,
  },
  'francisco-xavier': {
    nome: `Saint Francis Xavier`,
    resumo: `The great missionary who brought the Gospel to India, Japan and all of Asia.`,
    texto: `<p>A companion of Saint Ignatius of Loyola since their student days in Paris, Francis Xavier became one of the first and most ardent Jesuit missionaries, leaving for India in 1541 and never returning to Europe.</p>
<p>He traveled through Goa, the islands of Southeast Asia and Japan, learning local languages and customs to proclaim the faith and baptizing, according to tradition, a multitude of people. He died on the coast of China, still dreaming of evangelizing that immense country. He is considered the greatest missionary since apostolic times.</p>`,
  },
  'vicente-paulo': {
    nome: `Saint Vincent de Paul`,
    resumo: `The French priest who devoted his whole life to helping the poor and the abandoned.`,
    texto: `<p>The son of French peasants, Vincent had an ordinary priestly career until a series of encounters with rural poverty transformed his heart and his mission: from then on he lived entirely for the service of the poor, prisoners and the sick.</p>
<p>He founded the Congregation of the Mission (the Lazarist Fathers) and, together with Saint Louise de Marillac, the Daughters of Charity, one of the first women's orders to work outside the cloister, directly among those in need. He is the patron of all works of Christian charity.</p>`,
  },
  'bento-nursia': {
    nome: `Saint Benedict of Nursia`,
    resumo: `The father of Western monasticism, author of the Rule that still guides monasteries around the world.`,
    texto: `<p>Faced with the moral decay of Rome, the young Benedict withdrew to live in prayer and penance in an isolated cave. Little by little, disciples gathered around him, and so the first Benedictine monasteries were born, among them the famous Monte Cassino.</p>
<p>He wrote the Rule of Saint Benedict, marked by the motto "ora et labora" (pray and work), which balances prayer, study and manual labor with good sense and moderation. His twin sister, Saint Scholastica, followed a similar path, founding the female branch of monastic life. He is considered co-patron of Europe.</p>`,
  },
  'cecilia': {
    nome: `Saint Cecilia`,
    resumo: `Young Roman martyr, patron saint of musicians and sacred music.`,
    texto: `<p>A young woman from a noble Roman family, Cecilia had consecrated her heart to God, but was given in marriage against her will. Tradition says that during her own wedding feast, while the instruments played, she sang to God in her heart — an episode that, over time, made her the patron saint of musicians.</p>
<p>She managed to convert her husband and her brother-in-law to the Christian faith before they were all martyred during the Roman persecutions. Her tomb, rediscovered centuries later with her body intact, became one of the most visited in Rome.</p>`,
  },
  'sebastiao': {
    nome: `Saint Sebastian`,
    resumo: `Roman soldier martyred with arrows, a survivor who became a symbol of fortitude.`,
    texto: `<p>An officer in the army of Emperor Diocletian, Sebastian used his position to secretly help persecuted Christians, until he was discovered and condemned to die tied to a post and pierced with arrows by his own soldiers.</p>
<p>According to tradition, he survived thanks to the care of a devout woman named Irene, and once again presented himself before the emperor to denounce the persecution — and was then definitively martyred by being beaten to death. He is invoked as a protector against epidemics and is the patron saint of athletes and soldiers.</p>`,
  },
  'judas-tadeu': {
    nome: `Saint Jude Thaddeus`,
    resumo: `Apostle of Jesus, today the most popular intercessor for urgent and impossible causes.`,
    texto: `<p>One of the twelve apostles chosen by Jesus, Jude Thaddeus traveled through various regions preaching the Gospel after the Resurrection and Pentecost, and is traditionally also associated with Persia, where he is said to have been martyred alongside Saint Simon.</p>
<p>Because his name resembles that of Judas Iscariot, for centuries he was one of the least invoked apostles — which, according to popular devotion, made him especially willing to help those who turn to him in desperate situations. Today he is one of the most beloved saints in Brazil, invoked in urgent and impossible causes.</p>`,
  },
  'jorge': {
    nome: `Saint George`,
    resumo: `The martyr knight whose dragon legend symbolizes the victory of good over evil.`,
    texto: `<p>A Roman soldier of Greek origin, George served in the army of Emperor Diocletian until the wave of persecution against Christians forced him to choose between denying his faith or dying. He chose to remain faithful and was tortured and beheaded at the beginning of the 4th century.</p>
<p>The famous legend of the knight who faces a dragon to save a princess appeared centuries later, becoming a symbol of Christian courage in the face of evil. He is the patron of soldiers, scouts and countries such as Georgia and England, and one of the most fervent devotions in Rio de Janeiro.</p>`,
  },
  'cristovao': {
    nome: `Saint Christopher`,
    resumo: `The legendary giant who carried the Child Jesus and became the patron saint of travelers.`,
    texto: `<p>According to the beautiful tradition surrounding his story, Christopher was a man of extraordinary strength who decided to put himself at the service of the most powerful king he could find. A hermit advised him to serve God by carrying travelers across a dangerous river.</p>
<p>One night he carried on his shoulders a child who, in the middle of the crossing, grew heavier and heavier — it was Christ himself, who bore the weight of the world. Hence his name, "Christopher" (the one who carries Christ). He is invoked as the protector of travelers and drivers.</p>`,
  },
  'cosme-damiao': {
    nome: `Saints Cosmas and Damian`,
    resumo: `Twin brothers and physicians who healed free of charge in the name of Christ.`,
    texto: `<p>Born in Arabia, Cosmas and Damian studied medicine and began treating the sick who came to them free of charge, refusing any payment — which is why they became known as the "Anargyroi", those who heal without receiving anything in return. Their reputation for charity and skill spread quickly.</p>
<p>They were arrested and martyred during the persecutions of Emperor Diocletian, remaining faithful to the end. Their official liturgical feast is on September 26, but in Brazil popular devotion usually hands out sweets to children on September 27, in memory of their generosity.</p>`,
  },
  'rita-cassia': {
    nome: `Saint Rita of Cascia`,
    resumo: `The "saint of impossible cases", who endured a difficult marriage before entering religious life.`,
    texto: `<p>Rita dreamed of religious life from childhood, but obeyed her parents and married young to a violent and unfaithful man. She endured the marriage with patience and prayer for almost twenty years and managed, before his tragic death, to convert her husband's heart.</p>
<p>Widowed, she tried three times to enter an Augustinian convent until she was finally accepted. As a nun, she received on her forehead a painful wound, like a thorn from Christ's crown, which she bore until the end of her life. She became the great patron saint of cases considered impossible.</p>`,
  },
  'isabel-hungria': {
    nome: `Saint Elizabeth of Hungary`,
    resumo: `Hungarian princess who traded the luxury of the court for caring for the poor with her own hands.`,
    texto: `<p>The daughter of the King of Hungary, Elizabeth was raised at the court of Thuringia, where she married young, for love, Landgrave Louis. Despite her life of privilege, she personally devoted herself to bringing bread, clothing and care to the poor of the realm, which was not looked upon kindly by all the nobility.</p>
<p>According to tradition, one day her husband surprised her carrying bread hidden in her cloak for the needy; when she opened it before him, the loaves had turned into roses. Widowed while still young, she began living with Franciscan simplicity, building a hospital where she herself cared for the sick.</p>`,
  },
  'monica': {
    nome: `Saint Monica`,
    resumo: `The mother who, through decades of tears and prayer, obtained the conversion of her son Augustine.`,
    texto: `<p>Married to a pagan man with a difficult temperament, Monica lived her faith with patience and firmness and, over time, obtained the conversion of her husband and her mother-in-law. Her greatest battle, however, was accompanying her son, Augustine, through years of a scattered life, far from the Church and from the truth.</p>
<p>She followed him closely through several cities, weeping and praying unceasingly for his conversion, until she witnessed, in Milan, the moment when he finally turned to God, guided by Saint Ambrose. She is the great patron saint of mothers who persevere in prayer for their children.</p>`,
  },
  'expedito': {
    nome: `Saint Expeditus`,
    resumo: `The saint of urgent causes, invoked by those who need a quick answer.`,
    texto: `<p>Tradition says that Expeditus was a Roman soldier who, upon deciding to convert to Christianity, was tempted by the devil himself, disguised as a crow, to put off the decision until the next day. Expeditus is said to have stepped on the bird and answered: "This very day I will be a Christian!" — and so he did, without delay.</p>
<p>This determination not to put off what is urgent became his devotional hallmark: today he is one of the most invoked saints in Brazil for causes that cannot wait, always depicted holding a cross bearing the Latin word "Hodie" (today).</p>`,
  },
  'barbara': {
    nome: `Saint Barbara`,
    resumo: `Young martyr locked away by her own father, invoked against storms and sudden dangers.`,
    texto: `<p>According to tradition, Barbara was the daughter of a rich pagan who, afraid of losing control over her beauty and intelligence, kept her locked in a tower. It was there that she secretly came to know the Christian faith and decided to embrace it, having a third window opened in the tower in honor of the Holy Trinity.</p>
<p>On discovering his daughter's conversion, her own father handed her over to the authorities and, according to the popular account, was struck by lightning right after executing her. That is why Saint Barbara is invoked as a protector against storms, lightning and sudden death.</p>`,
  },
  'jose-anchieta': {
    nome: `Saint José de Anchieta`,
    resumo: `The "Apostle of Brazil", a Jesuit missionary who helped found São Paulo.`,
    texto: `<p>Born in the Canary Islands, José de Anchieta came to Brazil as a young Jesuit missionary, devoting himself body and soul to evangelizing the Indigenous peoples. He learned the Tupi language so deeply that he wrote its first grammar, as well as catechisms, poems and plays to teach the faith.</p>
<p>He took part in the founding of the College of São Paulo de Piratininga, the origin of the city of São Paulo, and helped seal peace between the Portuguese and the Tamoio people, offering himself as a hostage. He was canonized in 2014 by Pope Francis, becoming one of the great symbols of faith in colonial Brazil.</p>`,
  },
  'madre-paulina': {
    nome: `Saint Pauline of the Agonizing Heart of Jesus`,
    resumo: `An Italian immigrant who became the first person linked to Brazil to be canonized.`,
    texto: `<p>Amabile Lucia Visintainer was born in Italy and emigrated as a child with her family to Santa Catarina, in southern Brazil. From a young age she felt called to care for the poorest, and began taking in the sick in a small hut next to her home.</p>
<p>She founded the Congregation of the Little Sisters of the Immaculate Conception, taking the name Mother Pauline, and later expanded her work to São Paulo, caring for the sick, orphans and formerly enslaved people. She was canonized in 2002, becoming the first person with a direct connection to Brazil raised to the altars.</p>`,
  },
  'edith-stein': {
    nome: `Saint Teresa Benedicta of the Cross (Edith Stein)`,
    resumo: `A Jewish philosopher who converted to Catholicism, killed at Auschwitz for being both Christian and Jewish.`,
    texto: `<p>Born into a practicing Jewish family in Germany, Edith Stein became a brilliant philosopher, a disciple of the phenomenologist Edmund Husserl. After years of intellectual searching, reading the autobiography of Saint Teresa of Ávila in a single night completely changed the course of her life: she converted to Catholicism.</p>
<p>She entered the Carmel, taking the name Teresa Benedicta of the Cross, but with the Nazi persecution of the Jews she was arrested together with her sister and deported to the Auschwitz extermination camp, where she died in 1942. She is recognized as a martyr and co-patroness of Europe.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-bosco': {
    nome: `Saint John Bosco`,
    resumo: `The "father and teacher of youth", who devoted his life to educating poor young people.`,
    texto: `<p>Raised in a humble family in Piedmont, Italy, John Bosco knew firsthand the poverty and lack of opportunity faced by the young people of his time. Once a priest, he began gathering street boys to play, study and pray, in a project that became known as the Oratory.</p>
<p>He founded the Salesian Society, dedicated to the education and evangelization of young people through a method based on reason, religion and loving kindness — the so-called "preventive system". He was the spiritual mentor of young saints such as Dominic Savio, and left an educational legacy present in Salesian schools all over the world.</p>`,
  },
  'frei-galvao': {
    nome: `Saint Anthony of Saint Anne Galvão (Friar Galvão)`,
    resumo: `The first saint born on Brazilian soil, famous for his paper pills with prayers.`,
    texto: `<p>Born in Guaratinguetá, in the interior of São Paulo state, Friar Galvão entered the Franciscan Order and spent most of his life devoted to prayer, building work and spiritual direction in São Paulo. He founded the Retreat of Our Lady of Light, today the Monastery of Light (Mosteiro da Luz).</p>
<p>He became known for handwriting short prayers on strips of paper, given to the faithful as the famous "Friar Galvão pills", still distributed today and associated with healings and graces. Canonized in 2007, he became the first saint born on Brazilian soil.</p>`,
  },
});

// Lote 3 (setembro de 2026): mais 21 santos, na ordem do catálogo
Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en, {
  'teresinha': {
    nome: `Saint Thérèse of the Child Jesus`,
    resumo: `The young Carmelite who taught the world to reach Heaven through her "Little Way".`,
    texto: `<p>She entered the Carmel at a very young age and developed the "Little Way": doing the simple things of everyday life with extraordinary love. She died in 1897 at the age of 24, leaving her autobiography, "Story of a Soul", and was later declared a Doctor of the Church.</p>
<p>The youngest of nine children, she lost her mother at the age of four and was raised by her older sisters, who one by one also entered religious life. With a persistence remarkable for her age, she even asked Pope Leo XIII in person and obtained special permission to enter the Carmel at only fifteen. She lived a hidden, simple life inside the convent, offering every small gesture as a gift of love to God and teaching that great deeds are not needed to reach holiness. She died of tuberculosis while still young, promising to "spend her Heaven doing good on earth".</p>`,
  },
  'clara-assis': {
    nome: `Saint Clare of Assisi`,
    resumo: `The "little plant of Saint Francis", who founded the Order of Poor Clares with a radical love of poverty.`,
    texto: `<p>A faithful follower of Saint Francis, she ran away from home to consecrate her life to God in absolute poverty, founding the Order of Poor Clares and protecting her convent with the immense spiritual strength of the Blessed Sacrament.</p>
<p>Born into a noble family of Assisi, she heard Francis preach as a teenager and decided, at eighteen, to leave home one night to join him, and her hair was cut before the altar as a sign of consecration. She withstood the fury of her family, who tried to drag her back by force, and founded the women's branch of the Franciscan movement. In a famous episode, with the convent surrounded by Saracen soldiers, she stood at the door holding the consecrated Host — and the army, seized with fear, withdrew. She was the first woman in history to write a religious rule for other women.</p>`,
  },
  'carlo-acutis': {
    nome: `Saint Carlo Acutis`,
    resumo: `The young patron of the internet, in love with the Eucharist and with technology.`,
    texto: `<h2>The Patron of the Internet and of Love for the Eucharist</h2>
<p>From an early age, Carlo showed an intense faith, attending Mass every day. He was an ordinary boy who loved video games and programming. He used his passion for technology to create a website cataloguing Eucharistic miracles. He died of leukemia at the age of 15, offering his sufferings for the Church.</p>
<p>He was canonized on September 7, 2025, by Pope Leo XIV, together with Saint Pier Giorgio Frassati, becoming one of the first saints born in the 1990s. His feast, celebrated on October 12 — the day he died —, coincides in Brazil with the feast of Our Lady Aparecida, the country's patroness, a coincidence that many Brazilian devotees find full of meaning.</p>`,
  },
  'chiara-luce': {
    nome: `Blessed Chiara Luce Badano`,
    resumo: `A radiant young woman who turned the pain of terminal cancer into a proof of love for God.`,
    texto: `<p>Chiara was born in Italy in 1971. At 16 she was diagnosed with an aggressive bone cancer. Instead of rebelling, she embraced the cross with the words: "If You want it, Jesus, I want it too." She died at 18, leaving a witness of unshakeable joy.</p>
<p>Before her illness she was an ordinary teenager who loved tennis and dancing, involved since childhood in the Focolare Movement. A pain in her shoulder, felt during a tennis match, revealed the tumor. During her treatment she refused doses of morphine that would make her drowsy, saying she wanted to live every remaining moment fully aware. She welcomed friends and other sick young people with a constant smile, becoming known for her serenity in the face of pain. She was beatified in 2010, and is one of the faces of holiness closest to young people today.</p>`,
  },
  'pier-giorgio': {
    nome: `Saint Pier Giorgio Frassati`,
    resumo: `A young mountaineer who devoted his life and his means to the poor and sick of Turin.`,
    texto: `<p>Born into a wealthy family, Pier Giorgio stood out for his great joy, his passion for mountaineering and his deep social commitment. He secretly gave his savings to people in need, until he contracted poliomyelitis and died at the age of 24.</p>
<p>He was canonized on September 7, 2025, by Pope Leo XIV, in the same ceremony as Saint Carlo Acutis — the two patrons of World Youth Day Lisbon 2023 raised to the altars together, as models of everyday, "street-level" holiness for today's young people.</p>`,
  },
  'domingos-savio': {
    nome: `Saint Dominic Savio`,
    resumo: `A pupil of Don Bosco, one of the youngest non-martyr saints ever canonized.`,
    texto: `<p>The son of a humble blacksmith from Piedmont, Italy, Dominic was brought as a boy to study at Don Bosco's Oratory, and Don Bosco soon recognized in him an uncommon holiness. He took every gesture of daily life seriously, always trying to cheer up his companions and help anyone in need.</p>
<p>Among his companions he founded the "Company of the Immaculate Conception", encouraging other young people to live their faith naturally and joyfully. He died at 14, in 1857, and became a sign that holiness is also possible in childhood and adolescence.</p>`,
  },
  'maria-goretti': {
    nome: `Saint Maria Goretti`,
    resumo: `A young Italian peasant girl who, while still alive, forgave the man who attacked her.`,
    texto: `<p>The daughter of very poor farmworkers in the Italian countryside, Maria grew up helping to care for her younger siblings after her father's death. At 11, she bravely resisted an assault by a neighbor and was struck several times for refusing to give in.</p>
<p>Before dying in the hospital, she explicitly forgave her attacker and expressed the wish to meet him again one day in Heaven. Years later, having repented, he personally attended Maria's canonization ceremony in 1950. She is the patroness of young people and of purity of life.</p>`,
  },
  'jose-sanchez-rio': {
    nome: `Saint José Sánchez del Río`,
    resumo: `The Mexican boy who chose death rather than deny his faith, crying out "Long live Christ the King!".`,
    texto: `<p>During the violent religious persecution in Mexico in the 1920s, the teenage José insistently asked to join the Cristeros, lay people who resisted the laws that banned Catholic worship. Captured in combat at 14, he was tortured to make him renounce his faith and give up information.</p>
<p>He flatly refused, answering his executioners only with prayers and the cry "Long live Christ the King!". He was stabbed and finally shot in February 1928. Canonized in 2016, he is today the patron of altar servers and a symbol of young people's faithfulness to the faith.</p>`,
  },
  'kateri-tekakwitha': {
    nome: `Saint Kateri Tekakwitha`,
    resumo: `The "Lily of the Mohawks", the first Indigenous woman of North America to be canonized.`,
    texto: `<p>The daughter of a Mohawk chief and a Christian Algonquin mother, Kateri survived, as a small child, a smallpox epidemic that left her face scarred and her eyesight weak, and that also killed her parents. She grew up among relatives who strongly opposed her conversion to Christianity.</p>
<p>Despite persecution from her own community, she consecrated herself to God and devoted herself to a life of intense prayer and penance, and was called the "Lily of the Mohawks" for her purity. Witnesses reported that, when she died, the smallpox scars on her face disappeared. She was canonized in 2012, becoming the first Indigenous saint of North America.</p>`,
  },
  'pastorinhos-fatima': {
    nome: `Francisco and Jacinta Marto`,
    resumo: `The little shepherds of Fátima, witnesses of the apparitions of Our Lady in 1917.`,
    texto: `<p>Shepherd siblings from Fátima, Portugal, Francisco and Jacinta, together with their cousin Lúcia, witnessed in 1917 the apparitions of Our Lady at the Cova da Iria, receiving from her messages of prayer, penance and conversion that quickly spread through Portugal and the whole world.</p>
<p>After the apparitions, the two siblings generously offered their small daily sacrifices out of love for God and for sinners, living an intense life of prayer. They died as children, victims of the Spanish flu pandemic of 1918–1920, and in 2017 became the youngest non-martyr saints in the history of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'laura-vicuna': {
    nome: `Blessed Laura Vicuña`,
    resumo: `A young Chilean girl who offered her own life for her mother's conversion.`,
    texto: `<p>Born in Chile and raised in Argentina after her father's death, Laura saw up close her mother's suffering in an abusive relationship. Educated by Salesian sisters, she took the young Dominic Savio as her model, even before he was canonized.</p>
<p>Unable to change her family's situation in any other way, she offered her own life to God as a sacrifice for her mother's conversion. She fell gravely ill and died at 12, having obtained, according to accounts, her mother's return to the faith shortly before she died. She was beatified in 1988.</p>`,
  },
  'solanus-casey': {
    nome: `Blessed Solanus Casey`,
    resumo: `An American Capuchin friar, known for his simplicity and his gift of healing.`,
    texto: `<p>The son of Irish immigrants in the United States, Solanus struggled with his studies and, for that reason, was never allowed to preach or hear confessions like an ordinary priest. He then became the "porter priest" of his friary in Detroit, welcoming the visitors who came to its door.</p>
<p>It was precisely in this seemingly simple role that his holiness blossomed: thousands of people sought him out for advice, comfort and prayer for the sick, and many reported unexplained healings. He also opened a soup kitchen to feed the poorest during the Great Depression. He was beatified in 2017.</p>`,
  },
  'miguel-pro': {
    nome: `Blessed Miguel Pro`,
    resumo: `A Mexican Jesuit priest executed without trial during the religious persecution.`,
    texto: `<p>Ordained a Jesuit priest in the midst of the violent anticlerical persecution in Mexico, Miguel Pro carried out his ministry in secret, disguising himself in all sorts of ways to celebrate Masses, baptisms and confessions without being discovered by the authorities — often with good humor despite the constant danger.</p>
<p>Falsely accused of an assassination attempt he did not commit, he was sentenced to death without a formal trial. Before the firing squad he refused the blindfold, opened his arms in the shape of a cross and cried "Long live Christ the King!" at the moment of his death, in 1927. He was beatified in 1988.</p>`,
  },
  'basilio-magno': {
    nome: `Saint Basil the Great`,
    resumo: `A Greek bishop who organized Eastern monastic life and defended the faith against the Arians.`,
    texto: `<p>One of the great Fathers of the Eastern Church, Basil organized monastic life around communities devoted to prayer and work, and founded a vast complex of care for the poor and the sick in Caesarea. He firmly defended the divinity of Christ against the Arians and is considered a Doctor of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'isabel-ana-seton': {
    nome: `Saint Elizabeth Ann Seton`,
    resumo: `An American widow who founded the first parish schools in the United States.`,
    texto: `<p>Born into a Protestant family in New York, she converted to Catholicism after being widowed young, with five children to raise. She founded the first congregation of religious sisters born in the United States and organized the country's system of Catholic parish schools. She was the first person born in the United States to be canonized.</p>`,
  },
  'andre-bessette': {
    nome: `Saint André Bessette`,
    resumo: `A Canadian lay brother and convent porter, known for healings attributed to his prayer.`,
    texto: `<p>Frail in health and with little schooling, he was accepted as a lay brother by the Congregation of Holy Cross only for the simplest tasks — he spent decades as the porter of a school. His simple faith and his devotion to Saint Joseph drew crowds seeking healing, which led him to build Saint Joseph's Oratory in Montreal, today one of the largest shrines in the world.</p>`,
  },
  'raimundo-penafort': {
    nome: `Saint Raymond of Peñafort`,
    resumo: `A Spanish Dominican friar who organized the canon law of the Church.`,
    texto: `<p>A jurist by training, he joined the Dominicans as an adult and devoted himself to organizing and systematizing the laws of the Church, producing a collection of decretals that served as a reference for centuries. He also worked for the peaceful conversion of Muslims and Jews in Spain, encouraging the study of languages for the sake of dialogue.</p>`,
  },
  'hilario-poitiers': {
    nome: `Saint Hilary of Poitiers`,
    resumo: `A French bishop exiled for defending the divinity of Christ against Arianism.`,
    texto: `<p>Converted from paganism as an adult after studying the Scriptures on his own, he became bishop of Poitiers and one of the greatest defenders of faith in Christ as true God, which cost him years of exile imposed by the Arian emperor Constantius II. He is called the "Hammer of the Arians" and is a Doctor of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'antao-grande': {
    nome: `Saint Anthony the Great`,
    resumo: `The father of monks, who withdrew into the Egyptian desert in search of God.`,
    texto: `<p>After hearing at Mass the passage in which Jesus asks us to sell everything and follow him, Anthony gave his whole inheritance to the poor and withdrew into the desert of Egypt, living for decades in solitude, prayer and spiritual combat. Disciples gradually gathered around him, giving rise to Christian monastic life. He is invoked as a protector of animals.</p>`,
  },
  'ines-roma': {
    nome: `Saint Agnes of Rome`,
    resumo: `A young Roman martyr who preferred death to betraying her consecration to Christ.`,
    texto: `<p>While still a girl, she consecrated her virginity to Christ and refused several suitors from noble families, which led to her being denounced as a Christian during the Roman persecutions. She faced martyrdom with a serenity astonishing for her young age, becoming, from the first centuries of Christianity, one of the strongest symbols of purity and faithfulness.</p>`,
  },
  'conversao-paulo': {
    nome: `The Conversion of Saint Paul the Apostle`,
    resumo: `The persecutor of Christians struck down by the light of Christ on the road to Damascus.`,
    texto: `<p>A zealous Pharisee, Saul of Tarsus fiercely persecuted the first Christians until he was struck down by a light from heaven on the road to Damascus and heard the voice of Christ asking, "Why do you persecute me?". Blind for three days, he was baptized and became Paul, the greatest missionary in the history of Christianity, traveling across the Roman Empire and writing a large part of the New Testament.</p>`,
  },
});

// Lote 4 (setembro de 2026): mais 21 santos, na ordem do catálogo
Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en, {
  'angela-merici': {
    nome: `Saint Angela Merici`,
    resumo: `Founder of the first order devoted to the education of girls.`,
    texto: `<p>Orphaned while still young, Angela gathered consecrated laywomen devoted to prayer and teaching, founding the Company of Saint Ursula, the first institution in Europe dedicated specifically to the education of girls. Her pioneering vision that women could live a consecrated life outside the traditional cloister inspired generations of teaching congregations.</p>`,
  },
  'sao-bras': {
    nome: `Saint Blaise`,
    resumo: `A physician, bishop and martyr, invoked as protector of the throat.`,
    texto: `<p>A physician before becoming a bishop, Blaise withdrew to live as a hermit in a cave, where wild animals came to him to be healed. Arrested during the Roman persecutions, he is remembered for having saved, on his way to prison, a child who was choking on a fish bone — which is why he is still invoked today against illnesses of the throat.</p>`,
  },
  'santa-agueda': {
    nome: `Saint Agatha`,
    resumo: `A young Sicilian woman martyred for refusing the advances of a Roman governor.`,
    texto: `<p>From a noble family and consecrated to God from a young age, Agatha was pursued by a Roman governor who, after she rejected him, denounced her as a Christian during the persecution of Decius. She endured terrible tortures without renouncing her faith. She is the patroness of nurses and, because of her martyrdom, is invoked against diseases of the breast.</p>`,
  },
  'paulo-miki': {
    nome: `Saint Paul Miki and Companions`,
    resumo: `A Japanese Jesuit crucified with 25 companions during the persecution of Christians in Japan.`,
    texto: `<p>A young Japanese Jesuit seminarian, Paul Miki preached eloquently even on the way to his own martyrdom: he was crucified in Nagasaki alongside 25 other Christians — priests, religious and lay people, children among them — during the violent persecution of Christianity in Japan in 1597. From the cross, he still preached forgiveness to his executioners.</p>`,
  },
  'josefina-bakhita': {
    nome: `Saint Josephine Bakhita`,
    resumo: `Kidnapped and enslaved as a child in Sudan, she became a nun in Italy.`,
    texto: `<p>Kidnapped as a young girl in Sudan and sold as a slave, she suffered years of mistreatment until she was taken to Italy, where she finally came to know freedom and the Christian faith. She became a Canossian sister and lived the rest of her life with a serenity that impressed everyone, despite all she had suffered. She is the patroness of Sudan and a symbol of the fight against human trafficking.</p>`,
  },
  'cirilo-metodio': {
    nome: `Saints Cyril and Methodius`,
    resumo: `Missionary brothers who evangelized the Slavic peoples and created their alphabet.`,
    texto: `<p>Greek brothers sent as missionaries to the Slavic peoples, they created an alphabet of their own to translate the Bible and the liturgy into the language of the people — the origin of the Cyrillic alphabet still used today. They faced strong resistance from clergy who insisted on using Latin alone, but their work laid the foundations of the faith and of written culture among the Slavs. They are co-patrons of Europe.</p>`,
  },
  'pedro-damiao': {
    nome: `Saint Peter Damian`,
    resumo: `An Italian monk and cardinal who worked to reform the life of the clergy.`,
    texto: `<p>An orphan raised harshly by an older brother, he found in Benedictine monastic life the spiritual rigor he was seeking. He became an adviser to several popes and worked tirelessly to reform the discipline and morals of the clergy of his time, even when that meant confronting powerful bishops and cardinals. He was declared a Doctor of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'policarpo': {
    nome: `Saint Polycarp`,
    resumo: `A disciple of the apostle John, a martyr bishop condemned to the stake at 86 for refusing to deny Christ.`,
    texto: `<p>As a young man he was a direct disciple of the apostle John, becoming, as bishop of Smyrna, a living link between the apostolic generation and the Church that followed. In his old age he was arrested during a persecution and, when pressed to curse Christ to save his life, he answered: "Eighty-six years I have served him, and he has never done me any wrong — how can I blaspheme my King and Savior?" He was condemned to the stake.</p>`,
  },
  'cassimiro': {
    nome: `Saint Casimir`,
    resumo: `A Polish prince who turned down the throne and war for the sake of his faith.`,
    texto: `<p>The son of the king of Poland, he refused to lead a military invasion he considered unjust, preferring prayer to political ambition. He lived simply despite his royal birth, devoting himself to the poor and to devotion to Mary, and died young, of tuberculosis. He is the patron of Poland and Lithuania.</p>`,
  },
  'perpetua-felicidade': {
    nome: `Saints Perpetua and Felicity`,
    resumo: `A young noblewoman and her slave, martyred together in the arena of Carthage.`,
    texto: `<p>Perpetua, a young mother from a noble family, and Felicity, her pregnant slave, were arrested together in Carthage for being Christians and faced the arena side by side, refusing to renounce their faith even before the pleas of Perpetua's own father. The account of their martyrdom, partly written by Perpetua herself in prison, is one of the oldest Christian texts that have come down to us.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-de-deus': {
    nome: `Saint John of God`,
    resumo: `A Portuguese former soldier who devoted his life to caring for the sick poor.`,
    texto: `<p>After years as a soldier and a wandering life, a sermon by John of Ávila led him to a radical conversion. He began taking in and personally caring for the poorest and most abandoned sick people from the streets, founding hospitals that gave rise to the Hospitaller Order of Saint John of God, present today in dozens of countries. He is the patron of hospitals and nurses.</p>`,
  },
  'francisca-romana': {
    nome: `Saint Frances of Rome`,
    resumo: `A Roman noblewoman who joined the life of wife and mother with intense charity and mystical prayer.`,
    texto: `<p>Married as a teenager against her will, she became over time a devoted wife and mother, without giving up an intense spiritual life: it is said that she constantly saw her guardian angel. She founded a community of laywomen devoted to prayer and to helping the poor of Rome, showing that holiness also fits within ordinary family life.</p>`,
  },
  'sao-patricio': {
    nome: `Saint Patrick`,
    resumo: `Enslaved as a youth in Ireland, he returned as a missionary and converted it to Christianity.`,
    texto: `<p>Kidnapped by Irish pirates at sixteen and sold as a slave to tend sheep, Patrick lived years of solitude that deepened his faith. He managed to escape, but years later he felt called to return as a missionary to the very land of his captivity, evangelizing practically all of Ireland. He is the country's patron, and his feast is celebrated by Irish people all over the world.</p>`,
  },
  'cirilo-jerusalem': {
    nome: `Saint Cyril of Jerusalem`,
    resumo: `Bishop of Jerusalem, exiled three times for defending the orthodox faith against the Arians.`,
    texto: `<p>Bishop of the holy city of Jerusalem, Cyril devoted himself above all to instructing catechumens, leaving catecheses that are today a precious source on the faith and liturgy of the early centuries. His fidelity to orthodox doctrine cost him three exiles imposed by Arian factions, but he always returned to his see. He is a Doctor of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'turibio-mogrovejo': {
    nome: `Saint Turibius of Mogrovejo`,
    resumo: `A Spanish bishop who traveled colonial Peru on foot, evangelizing and defending the Indigenous peoples.`,
    texto: `<p>A lay jurist unexpectedly sent as archbishop of Lima, Turibius traveled for decades, on horseback and on foot, across an immense territory of colonial Peru, learning Indigenous languages and denouncing the colonizers' abuses against the native population. According to tradition, he confirmed future saints such as Rose of Lima and Martin de Porres.</p>`,
  },
  'francisco-paula': {
    nome: `Saint Francis of Paola`,
    resumo: `An Italian hermit, founder of the Minims, famous for crossing the sea on his own cloak.`,
    texto: `<p>While still a boy he lived as a hermit in a cave, beginning a life of radical austerity that drew disciples and gave rise to the Order of Minims. Popular tradition tells that, turned away by a boatman, he crossed the Strait of Messina on his own cloak spread out on the water. He became a spiritual adviser to French kings.</p>`,
  },
  'isidoro-sevilha': {
    nome: `Saint Isidore of Seville`,
    resumo: `A Spanish bishop who gathered all the knowledge of his time into an encyclopedia.`,
    texto: `<p>One of the last great scholars of the ancient world, Isidore wrote the "Etymologies", a vast encyclopedia that gathered and preserved Greco-Roman knowledge through the Middle Ages, when so many ancient works were being lost. As bishop of Seville, he also devoted himself to organizing the Hispanic liturgy. Because of his work, he is regarded as the patron of the internet.</p>`,
  },
  'vicente-ferrer': {
    nome: `Saint Vincent Ferrer`,
    resumo: `A Spanish Dominican friar, an itinerant preacher who traveled across Europe calling people to conversion.`,
    texto: `<p>A Dominican of extraordinary eloquence, Vincent Ferrer spent the last twenty years of his life traveling on foot through Spain, France, Italy and Switzerland, preaching in the open air to crowds about conversion and the Last Judgment. It is said that, although he preached only in Valencian, he was understood by listeners who spoke other languages.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-batista-lasalle': {
    nome: `Saint John Baptist de La Salle`,
    resumo: `A French priest who founded free schools for poor boys.`,
    texto: `<p>A priest from a noble family, he gave up church benefices and his own fortune to devote himself entirely to the education of poor boys, creating a method of teaching in classes (instead of individual lessons) that would become the standard in schools. He founded the Brothers of the Christian Schools. He is regarded as the patron of teachers.</p>`,
  },
  'estanislau': {
    nome: `Saint Stanislaus`,
    resumo: `A Polish bishop martyred by the king himself for denouncing his injustices.`,
    texto: `<p>Bishop of Kraków, known for his courage in publicly denouncing the injustices committed by King Bolesław II, he even excommunicated him. In retaliation, he was killed by the king himself while celebrating Mass. He became a symbol of the freedom of the Church in the face of political power and is a patron of Poland.</p>`,
  },
  'anselmo': {
    nome: `Saint Anselm`,
    resumo: `An Italian Benedictine monk, considered the father of scholastic philosophy.`,
    texto: `<p>A Benedictine monk who became archbishop of Canterbury, Anselm is remembered as a pioneer of the scholastic method, seeking to understand the truths of faith through reason — he summed up his quest in the motto "faith seeking understanding". He endured two exiles for defending the independence of the Church from the English kings.</p>`,
  },
});

// Lote 5 (setembro de 2026): mais 21 santos, na ordem do catálogo
Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en, {
  'fidelis-sigmaringen': {
    nome: `Saint Fidelis of Sigmaringen`,
    resumo: `A German lawyer who became a Capuchin friar and a missionary martyr.`,
    texto: `<p>A brilliant and successful lawyer, Fidelis gave up his career when he saw the corruption in the courts and became a Capuchin friar devoted to the poor. Sent as a missionary to reconcile Calvinist Protestants with the Church in Switzerland, he was ambushed and killed by a group hostile to his preaching.</p>`,
  },
  'marcos-evangelista': {
    nome: `Saint Mark the Evangelist`,
    resumo: `Author of the second Gospel, disciple of Peter and founder of the Church of Alexandria.`,
    texto: `<p>A companion of Paul and Barnabas and, above all, Peter's interpreter in Rome, Mark put the apostle's preaching into writing, giving rise to the oldest Gospel in the New Testament. A strong tradition identifies him as the founder of the Church of Alexandria, in Egypt, where he is said to have been martyred. He is represented by a winged lion and is the patron of Venice.</p>`,
  },
  'jose-operario': {
    nome: `Saint Joseph the Worker`,
    resumo: `The same Saint Joseph, celebrated on this day as the patron of workers.`,
    texto: `<p>Instituted by Pope Pius XII in 1955, this second feast of Saint Joseph in the calendar — on Labor Day — celebrates in particular his dedication to manual work as a carpenter in Nazareth, presenting him as the model and protector of all laborers and workers in the world.</p>`,
  },
  'atanasio': {
    nome: `Saint Athanasius`,
    resumo: `Bishop of Alexandria, a tireless defender of the divinity of Christ against Arianism.`,
    texto: `<p>While still a young deacon, he took part in the Council of Nicaea, defending that Christ is truly God, against the Arian heresy that denied it. As bishop of Alexandria he was exiled five times by different emperors for his doctrinal firmness, even spending years hidden in the desert among monks. He is one of the greatest Doctors of the Eastern Church.</p>`,
  },
  'filipe-tiago': {
    nome: `Saints Philip and James, Apostles`,
    resumo: `Two of the twelve apostles of Jesus, celebrated together since ancient times.`,
    texto: `<p>Philip was the one who introduced Nathanael to Jesus and, at the Last Supper, asked Christ: "Show us the Father, and that will be enough for us" — receiving the answer that whoever sees Jesus sees the Father. James, called "the Less", led the Church of Jerusalem and is traditionally identified as the author of the Letter of James. Both gave their lives preaching the Gospel after Pentecost.</p>`,
  },
  'matias-apostolo': {
    nome: `Saint Matthias the Apostle`,
    resumo: `Chosen by lot to replace Judas Iscariot among the twelve apostles.`,
    texto: `<p>According to the Acts of the Apostles, after the betrayal and death of Judas Iscariot, the Christian community chose by lot, among the disciples who had followed Jesus from the beginning, the one who would once again complete the number of twelve apostles — and the lot fell on Matthias. Tradition places his preaching and martyrdom in the region of present-day Georgia.</p>`,
  },
  'isidro-lavrador': {
    nome: `Saint Isidore the Farmer`,
    resumo: `A Spanish peasant known for his life of constant prayer while working the land.`,
    texto: `<p>A humble farmworker in Madrid, Isidore was known for praying continually as he plowed the land, and popular accounts say that angels helped him with the plow while he prayed. Married to Saint Maria de la Cabeza, he lived a simple life of work, prayer and charity toward the poorest. He is the patron of farmers.</p>`,
  },
  'bernardino-sena': {
    nome: `Saint Bernardine of Siena`,
    resumo: `An Italian Franciscan friar famous for his preaching and his devotion to the name of Jesus.`,
    texto: `<p>After caring for the sick during an epidemic of plague, he caught the disease himself and, once he recovered, decided to join the Franciscans. He became one of the greatest popular preachers of his time, spreading devotion to the letters "IHS" (the name of Jesus in Greek) as a symbol of reconciliation between rival families and cities.</p>`,
  },
  'beda-veneravel': {
    nome: `Saint Bede the Venerable`,
    resumo: `An English monk who wrote the first great history of the Church in England.`,
    texto: `<p>Entrusted as a boy to a monastery in Northumbria, Bede practically never left it, devoting his whole life to study, teaching and writing. His "Ecclesiastical History of the English People" is the main source on the first centuries of Christianity in England. He was also one of the first to popularize counting the years from the birth of Christ.</p>`,
  },
  'filipe-neri': {
    nome: `Saint Philip Neri`,
    resumo: `The "saint of joy", who evangelized Rome with good humor and playfulness.`,
    texto: `<p>Known for his good-humored and playful manner, Philip Neri evangelized the young people of Rome by gathering them for prayer, music and outings, convinced that joy was a path to holiness, not an obstacle. He founded the Oratory, a congregation of secular priests devoted to spiritual formation through informal gatherings. He is a patron of Rome and of humor.</p>`,
  },
  'justino-martir': {
    nome: `Saint Justin Martyr`,
    resumo: `A converted pagan philosopher, the first great Christian apologist before Greek culture.`,
    texto: `<p>A Greek philosopher who went through several schools of thought in search of the truth, he finally found it in Christianity through meeting an elderly Christian. He kept wearing the philosopher's cloak, now to defend and explain the Christian faith to Greco-Roman culture in his "Apologies". He was beheaded in Rome for refusing to sacrifice to the pagan gods.</p>`,
  },
  'carlos-lwanga': {
    nome: `Saint Charles Lwanga and Companions`,
    resumo: `Young Ugandan martyrs, burned alive for refusing to abandon their faith.`,
    texto: `<p>Pages at the court of the king of Buganda, Charles Lwanga and a group of other young Christians were condemned to death for refusing both to abandon their faith and to give in to the king's own advances. Many were burned alive while still teenagers, facing martyrdom with a courage that impressed the whole region and spurred the growth of Christianity in Africa.</p>`,
  },
  'bonifacio': {
    nome: `Saint Boniface`,
    resumo: `An English monk, the "Apostle of Germany", martyred in old age while evangelizing Germanic peoples.`,
    texto: `<p>An English Benedictine monk who spent decades evangelizing the Germanic peoples, organizing dioceses and monasteries throughout the region. He became famous for personally cutting down a sacred oak of the pagans at Geismar without any divine lightning stopping him, which led to many conversions. He was killed in old age by a hostile band while waiting for a group of catechumens to be confirmed.</p>`,
  },
  'barnabe-apostolo': {
    nome: `Saint Barnabas the Apostle`,
    resumo: `Paul's companion on the first missionary journeys, called an apostle by tradition.`,
    texto: `<p>A Levite from Cyprus who sold his land to lay everything at the feet of the apostles, Barnabas was the one who introduced the newly converted Paul to the Christian community of Jerusalem, which was still wary of him. He became Paul's companion on the first great missionary journeys, and is called an "apostle" even though he was not part of the original group of twelve.</p>`,
  },
  'luis-gonzaga': {
    nome: `Saint Aloysius Gonzaga`,
    resumo: `A young Italian nobleman who gave up the title of marquis to serve plague victims.`,
    texto: `<p>Heir to an Italian marquisate, Aloysius renounced the succession and joined the Jesuits as a teenager, against his father's will. During an epidemic in Rome, he devoted himself to carrying and personally caring for dying patients gathered from the streets, catching the disease that killed him at 23. He is the patron of youth.</p>`,
  },
  'tomas-more': {
    nome: `Saint Thomas More`,
    resumo: `Lord Chancellor of England, beheaded by Henry VIII for not accepting the king as head of the Church.`,
    texto: `<p>A lawyer, writer and chancellor to King Henry VIII, Thomas More was one of the most respected men of his time. He resigned from office and was later imprisoned for refusing to swear loyalty to the king as the new head of the Church in England, denying the validity of the royal divorce. He went to the scaffold saying he died "the King's good servant, but God's first".</p>`,
  },
  'nascimento-joao-batista': {
    nome: `The Birth of Saint John the Baptist`,
    resumo: `The forerunner of Christ, who "leaped for joy" in his mother's womb before Mary.`,
    texto: `<p>The son of Elizabeth and Zechariah, conceived in his parents' old age as a sign of God's power, John is the only saint, besides Jesus and Mary, whose birth the Church celebrates with its own feast — because he was sanctified while still in his mother's womb, when he "leaped for joy" at hearing the greeting of the pregnant Mary. He became the prophet who prepared the way for the Messiah.</p>`,
  },
  'ireneu-lyon': {
    nome: `Saint Irenaeus of Lyon`,
    resumo: `A Greek bishop settled in Gaul, a great opponent of the Gnostic heresies.`,
    texto: `<p>A disciple of Polycarp, who in turn had been a disciple of the apostle John, Irenaeus became bishop of Lyon, in Gaul, and devoted his work "Against Heresies" to systematically refuting Gnosticism, defending the unity of the faith handed down by the apostles. He also worked for peace between Rome and the Churches of the East. He was declared a Doctor of the Church in 2022, with the title "Doctor of Unity".</p>`,
  },
  'pedro-paulo-apostolos': {
    nome: `Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles`,
    resumo: `The two greatest pillars of the early Church, celebrated together since the first centuries.`,
    texto: `<p>Peter, the fisherman Jesus called the "rock" on which he would build his Church, and Paul, the persecutor who became the greatest missionary, are celebrated on the same day because together they are the two greatest pillars of the early Church — both martyred in Rome under the emperor Nero, Peter crucified upside down because he considered himself unworthy to die as Christ did.</p>`,
  },
  'junipero-serra': {
    nome: `Saint Junípero Serra`,
    resumo: `A Spanish Franciscan friar, founder of the missions that gave rise to cities in California.`,
    texto: `<p>A professor of philosophy in Spain, Junípero Serra left academic life to become a missionary in the Americas when he was already nearly forty. Despite an injured leg that troubled him for the rest of his life, he walked thousands of kilometers founding missions in California that gave rise to cities such as San Diego and San Francisco.</p>`,
  },
  'tome-apostolo': {
    nome: `Saint Thomas the Apostle`,
    resumo: `The apostle who doubted the resurrection until he touched the wounds of Christ.`,
    texto: `<p>He became known as "the doubter" for refusing to believe the other apostles' account of the resurrection without seeing and touching the wounds of Jesus with his own hands — a request Christ granted, leading Thomas to the famous confession "My Lord and my God!". Tradition places his missionary work in India, where he is said to have founded Christian communities that still exist today.</p>`,
  },
});

// Lote 6 (setembro de 2026): mais 21 santos, na ordem do catálogo
Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en, {
  'henrique-imperador': {
    nome: `Saint Henry`,
    resumo: `A Germanic emperor who governed by placing faith and the Church above personal power.`,
    texto: `<p>Emperor of the Holy Roman Empire, Henry used his power to found dioceses, monasteries and churches, and is remembered for having lived, together with his wife Saint Cunigunde, a marriage wholly dedicated to God. He worked to reform church discipline in his time, and is a rare example of a ruler canonized for the holiness of his own government.</p>`,
  },
  'lourenco-brindes': {
    nome: `Saint Lawrence of Brindisi`,
    resumo: `A polyglot Capuchin friar, preacher and diplomat in the service of peace in Europe.`,
    texto: `<p>Gifted with an extraordinary ease for languages — he spoke about ten fluently —, Lawrence of Brindisi preached in each region in the people's own language. He served as a papal diplomat at several European courts, helping to organize Christian resistance against Ottoman invasions. He was declared a Doctor of the Church in 1959.</p>`,
  },
  'maria-madalena': {
    nome: `Saint Mary Magdalene`,
    resumo: `The first witness of the resurrection, called the "apostle to the apostles".`,
    texto: `<p>Set free by Jesus from deep suffering, Mary Magdalene became one of his most faithful followers, remaining at the foot of the cross when almost all the apostles had fled. She was the first person to meet the risen Jesus and was sent by him to announce the news to the apostles — which is why, since the first centuries, she has been called the "apostle to the apostles".</p>`,
  },
  'brigida-suecia': {
    nome: `Saint Bridget of Sweden`,
    resumo: `A Swedish noblewoman and mystic, a mother who, once widowed, founded a new religious order.`,
    texto: `<p>Married and the mother of eight children, among them Saint Catherine of Sweden, Bridget lived decades of intense family life before, as a widow, devoting herself entirely to the mystical life and founding the Order of the Most Holy Savior. She recorded numerous revelations and mystical visions about the Passion of Christ in her writings. She is a co-patroness of Europe.</p>`,
  },
  'joaquim-ana': {
    nome: `Saints Joachim and Anne`,
    resumo: `The parents of the Virgin Mary and grandparents of Jesus, according to Christian tradition.`,
    texto: `<p>Not mentioned directly in the Bible, Joachim and Anne are known through ancient Christian tradition as the parents of Mary, who are said to have waited many years for a child before receiving, already elderly, the news of her coming. They are venerated as the grandparents of Jesus and as patrons of grandparents and families.</p>`,
  },
  'marta-maria-lazaro': {
    nome: `Saints Martha, Mary and Lazarus`,
    resumo: `The three siblings of Bethany, close friends of Jesus, who raised Lazarus from the dead.`,
    texto: `<p>Martha, Mary and Lazarus, of Bethany, were one of the families closest to Jesus, who used to stay in their home. Martha is remembered for her practical zeal in serving, Mary for sitting at Jesus' feet to listen to him, and Lazarus for the greatest of miracles: he was raised by Jesus four days after his death, in front of a crowd.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-maria-vianney': {
    nome: `Saint John Mary Vianney`,
    resumo: `The "Curé of Ars", a humble priest who spent up to 16 hours a day in the confessional.`,
    texto: `<p>Considered a weak student at the seminary, he was almost not ordained, yet he became one of the greatest confessors in the history of the Church. Sent to the poor parish of Ars, in France, where faith had grown cold, he transformed it completely, drawing more than a hundred thousand pilgrims a year who came from all over Europe just to go to confession with him. He is the patron of priests.</p>`,
  },
  'domingos-gusmao': {
    nome: `Saint Dominic de Guzmán`,
    resumo: `Founder of the Order of Preachers (Dominicans), devoted to study and preaching.`,
    texto: `<p>A Spanish canon who, on encountering the Cathar heresy in the south of France, realized that it could only be answered with well-grounded preaching and a life of radical poverty — not with force. He founded the Order of Preachers, the Dominicans, joining deep theological study to the mendicant life. He is also traditionally associated with spreading the Holy Rosary.</p>`,
  },
  'maximiliano-kolbe': {
    nome: `Saint Maximilian Kolbe`,
    resumo: `A Polish Franciscan friar who offered to die in place of a father at Auschwitz.`,
    texto: `<p>A Franciscan friar and journalist, he was arrested by the Nazis and sent to the Auschwitz concentration camp. When a prisoner was chosen to be starved to death as punishment for an escape, Kolbe offered to die in his place, saying he was a priest and had no family. He survived for weeks in the starvation bunker and was finally killed by lethal injection. He is the patron of journalists and of families in difficulty.</p>`,
  },
  'bernardo-claraval': {
    nome: `Saint Bernard of Clairvaux`,
    resumo: `A Cistercian monk, the "Mellifluous Doctor", adviser to popes and a great devotee of Mary.`,
    texto: `<p>He entered the monastery of Cîteaux bringing with him some thirty relatives and friends, such was his power of persuasion. He founded the abbey of Clairvaux, from which dozens of new Cistercian monasteries would spread across Europe. He was an adviser to popes and kings, and his tender devotion to the Virgin Mary earned him the nickname "Mellifluous Doctor", for sermons as sweet as honey.</p>`,
  },
  'pio-x': {
    nome: `Saint Pius X`,
    resumo: `A pope of peasant origin, known for lowering the age of children's First Communion.`,
    texto: `<p>Born into a humble family of Italian peasants, Giuseppe Sarto became pope without ever losing the simplicity of his origins. He is remembered above all for lowering the minimum age for First Communion, understanding that children should not have to wait so long to receive the Eucharist, and for reforming sacred music and the catechism of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'rosa-lima': {
    nome: `Saint Rose of Lima`,
    resumo: `The first saint of the Americas, a Peruvian mystic of austere life devoted to the sick.`,
    texto: `<p>Extraordinarily beautiful, which she saw as an obstacle to the spiritual life she desired, Rose lived a life of intense penance and prayer in Lima, devoting herself to caring for children, the elderly and sick Indigenous people in a small room she built in the garden of her home. She was the first person born in the Americas to be canonized, in 1671.</p>`,
  },
  'bartolomeu-apostolo': {
    nome: `Saint Bartholomew the Apostle`,
    resumo: `One of the twelve apostles, traditionally identified with Nathanael, flayed alive for the faith.`,
    texto: `<p>Many scholars identify Bartholomew with Nathanael, the one Jesus described as "an Israelite in whom there is no deceit" when Philip introduced him. Tradition places his missionary preaching in distant lands such as Armenia and India, where he is said to have been martyred by being flayed alive — the martyrdom for which he is often depicted in art holding his own skin.</p>`,
  },
  'luis-ix-franca': {
    nome: `Saint Louis IX of France`,
    resumo: `A king of France who governed with deep justice and Christian devotion.`,
    texto: `<p>Crowned king while still a boy, Louis IX governed France for decades, joining political firmness to a deep faith, personally hearing the complaints of the humblest subjects beneath an oak tree and reforming the laws of the kingdom in the name of justice. He took part in crusades and is remembered as a rare model of a ruler who exercised power in the service of the common good.</p>`,
  },
  'pedro-claver': {
    nome: `Saint Peter Claver`,
    resumo: `A Spanish Jesuit who devoted his life to welcoming enslaved people newly arrived in the Americas.`,
    texto: `<p>A Jesuit missionary in colonial Colombia, Peter Claver personally went aboard the slave ships as soon as they docked in Cartagena, bringing water, food and medical care to the enslaved Africans crowded together in inhuman conditions. He declared himself "the slave of the slaves forever", baptizing thousands of people and defending their dignity over four decades.</p>`,
  },
  'cornelio-cipriano': {
    nome: `Saints Cornelius and Cyprian`,
    resumo: `A pope and a bishop, friends and martyrs, who faced persecution and schism together.`,
    texto: `<p>Cornelius, pope in Rome, and Cyprian, bishop of Carthage, kept up an intense correspondence and friendship while facing together one of the greatest crises of the early Church: how to welcome back Christians who had denied the faith under persecution. Both were martyred for their firm and charitable leadership in those difficult times.</p>`,
  },
  'sao-januario': {
    nome: `Saint Januarius`,
    resumo: `A martyr bishop and patron of Naples, whose blood kept as a relic still intrigues scientists by liquefying.`,
    texto: `<p>Bishop of Benevento, in Italy, he was beheaded during the persecution of Diocletian for refusing to renounce his faith. He became famous for a phenomenon repeated for centuries in Naples: a vial of his blood, kept as a relic, appears to liquefy on specific dates of the year, drawing the faithful and scientific curiosity to this day.</p>`,
  },
  'andre-kim-taegon': {
    nome: `Saint Andrew Kim Taegon and Companions`,
    resumo: `The first Korean priest, martyred along with more than a hundred lay companions.`,
    texto: `<p>The son of a family that had secretly converted to Christianity in a Korea hostile to the Christian faith, Andrew Kim became the first Korean-born priest, ordained after studying in secret abroad. He was beheaded at 25, a few years after his own father's martyrdom, and is celebrated together with more than a hundred Korean martyrs, most of them lay people, who gave their lives between the 18th and 19th centuries.</p>`,
  },
  'mateus-apostolo': {
    nome: `Saint Matthew the Apostle`,
    resumo: `A tax collector who left everything when Jesus called him, becoming an apostle and evangelist.`,
    texto: `<p>A tax collector in the service of Rome — a profession despised by the Jews of his time for serving the occupier —, Matthew was sitting at his tax booth when Jesus simply said "Follow me", and he left everything at once. He became an apostle and the author of the first Gospel, written especially to show the Jews that Jesus was the Messiah promised in the Scriptures.</p>`,
  },
  'anjos-guarda': {
    nome: `The Holy Guardian Angels`,
    resumo: `The belief that each person receives an angel of their own, appointed by God to protect them.`,
    texto: `<p>Since the first centuries, the Church has taught that each person receives from God an angel of their own, charged with guiding and protecting them throughout their earthly life. Jesus himself refers to this belief in the Gospel, saying that the angels of little children "always behold the face of my Father who is in heaven". It is a custom to pray every day to one's guardian angel, asking for protection.</p>`,
  },
  'sao-bruno': {
    nome: `Saint Bruno`,
    resumo: `A German teacher who left everything to live in silence and founded the Carthusians.`,
    texto: `<p>A respected teacher of theology in Reims, Bruno left a successful academic life to withdraw, with a few companions, to an isolated valley in the French Alps, giving rise to the Carthusian Order — devoted to a rare way of life of almost total silence and contemplative solitude, combining the hermit's life with life in community.</p>`,
  },
});

// Lote 7 (setembro de 2026): os últimos 21 santos do catálogo
Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en, {
  'margarida-maria-alacoque': {
    nome: `Saint Margaret Mary Alacoque`,
    resumo: `A French nun to whom Jesus revealed devotion to his Sacred Heart.`,
    texto: `<p>A French religious of the Visitation, she reported receiving several apparitions of Jesus revealing his Sacred Heart as a symbol of his infinite love for humanity, and asking that this devotion be spread throughout the Church. She faced years of distrust and opposition, even from her own superiors, before the devotion was recognized and spread across the world.</p>`,
  },
  'lucas-evangelista': {
    nome: `Saint Luke the Evangelist`,
    resumo: `A Greek physician, companion of Paul, author of the third Gospel and of the Acts of the Apostles.`,
    texto: `<p>A physician of Greek education and the only evangelist who was not Jewish, Luke accompanied Paul on several of his missionary journeys, described in rich detail in the Acts of the Apostles, which he also wrote. His Gospel pays special attention to the poor, to women and to God's mercy. He is regarded as the patron of physicians and also of artists, because of an ancient tradition that he was a painter.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-brebeuf': {
    nome: `Saint John de Brébeuf and Companions`,
    resumo: `Jesuit missionaries martyred among the Indigenous peoples of Canada.`,
    texto: `<p>A French Jesuit of frail health but extraordinary willpower, John de Brébeuf spent decades on mission among the Huron in what is today Canada, learning their language and customs in depth. He was captured and tortured to death together with other Jesuit missionaries during conflicts between rival Indigenous peoples. They are the first martyrs of Canada.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-capistrano': {
    nome: `Saint John of Capistrano`,
    resumo: `A Franciscan friar and former judge who, in old age, led the defense of Belgrade against an Ottoman invasion.`,
    texto: `<p>A successful judge before becoming a Franciscan friar, John of Capistrano became an itinerant preacher famous for drawing enormous crowds. At the age of 70, he helped organize and spiritually lead the Christian resistance that defended the city of Belgrade from a great Ottoman invasion in 1456, dying a few months after the victory.</p>`,
  },
  'martin-porres': {
    nome: `Saint Martin de Porres`,
    resumo: `A Peruvian Dominican friar, son of a formerly enslaved woman, devoted to the poor, the sick and animals.`,
    texto: `<p>The son of a Spanish nobleman and a freed Afro-Peruvian woman, Martin faced the racial prejudice of his time even inside the Dominican convent, where he was at first accepted only as a lay helper. He devoted his life to caring for the poorest sick people of Lima, without distinction of origin, and is also remembered for his extraordinary care for animals.</p>`,
  },
  'carlos-borromeu': {
    nome: `Saint Charles Borromeo`,
    resumo: `Cardinal and archbishop of Milan, a central figure in the reform of the Church after the Council of Trent.`,
    texto: `<p>The nephew of a pope, he became a cardinal and archbishop of Milan while still young, but devoted himself with unusual seriousness to the reform of the Church proposed by the Council of Trent, creating seminaries for the better formation of priests and reorganizing the pastoral life of the diocese. During a plague in Milan, he stayed in the city, personally caring for the sick when many had fled.</p>`,
  },
  'leao-magno': {
    nome: `Saint Leo the Great`,
    resumo: `The pope who persuaded Attila the Hun not to invade Rome, and defended the doctrine about Christ.`,
    texto: `<p>One of the most influential popes of antiquity, Leo is remembered for having gone out in person to meet Attila the Hun, persuading him not to march on Rome. His doctrinal letter on the two natures of Christ, human and divine, was decisive at the Council of Chalcedon. He is a Doctor of the Church and is called "Magnus" — the Great.</p>`,
  },
  'martinho-tours': {
    nome: `Saint Martin of Tours`,
    resumo: `A Roman soldier who shared his cloak with a beggar and later became a bishop.`,
    texto: `<p>While still a Roman soldier, Martin cut his own military cloak in half to share it with a beggar freezing in the cold on a winter night; that same night, he dreamed of Christ wearing the half of the cloak he had given away. He left the army, became a monk and later bishop of Tours, one of the first saints venerated without having been a martyr.</p>`,
  },
  'josafat': {
    nome: `Saint Josaphat`,
    resumo: `A Ukrainian bishop of the Eastern rite, martyred for his work for the unity of the Church.`,
    texto: `<p>A monk and later a bishop of the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church, Josaphat worked tirelessly to promote communion between Eastern Christians of the Byzantine rite and Rome, which met strong resistance from groups opposed to the union. He was murdered by a hostile mob while visiting a parish, becoming the first Eastern saint formally canonized by Rome.</p>`,
  },
  'frances-cabrini': {
    nome: `Saint Frances Xavier Cabrini`,
    resumo: `An Italian nun who founded dozens of institutions for immigrants in the United States.`,
    texto: `<p>Although she was afraid of water after nearly drowning as a child, Francesca Cabrini crossed the ocean dozens of times to serve poor Italian immigrants in the United States, founding schools, orphanages and hospitals across the country. She became an American citizen and was the first citizen of the United States to be canonized.</p>`,
  },
  'gertrudes': {
    nome: `Saint Gertrude`,
    resumo: `A German mystic nun, one of the great promoters of devotion to the Heart of Jesus.`,
    texto: `<p>Entrusted as a child to a Benedictine monastery in Germany, Gertrude received an excellent intellectual formation before experiencing, as an adult, a deep mystical conversion that led her to write down her experiences of union with Christ. Her writings anticipated by centuries the devotion to the Sacred Heart of Jesus, later spread by Saint Margaret Mary Alacoque.</p>`,
  },
  'andre-dung-lac': {
    nome: `Saint Andrew Dung-Lac and Companions`,
    resumo: `A Vietnamese priest, celebrated together with more than a hundred martyrs of Vietnam.`,
    texto: `<p>A Vietnamese priest executed during decades of intense persecution of Christians in Vietnam, Andrew Dung-Lac is celebrated together with more than a hundred other martyrs — Vietnamese priests, religious and lay people, as well as foreign missionaries — who gave their lives between the 17th and 19th centuries, refusing to trample on the cross to prove they had renounced their faith.</p>`,
  },
  'catarina-alexandria': {
    nome: `Saint Catherine of Alexandria`,
    resumo: `A young Egyptian scholar who debated and converted pagan philosophers before her martyrdom.`,
    texto: `<p>According to tradition, Catherine was a young woman of extraordinary intelligence in Alexandria who, still a teenager, confronted the emperor for persecuting Christians, debating with a group of pagan philosophers sent to refute her and converting them to the faith. She refused to marry the emperor and was martyred. She is the patroness of students, philosophers and librarians.</p>`,
  },
  'andre-apostolo': {
    nome: `Saint Andrew the Apostle`,
    resumo: `The first apostle called by Jesus, brother of Peter, crucified on an X-shaped cross.`,
    texto: `<p>A fisherman like his brother Simon Peter, Andrew was a disciple of John the Baptist before becoming one of the first to follow Jesus, which is why he is called "Protokletos" (the first called). Tradition places his preaching and martyrdom in Greece, crucified on an X-shaped cross that today bears his name. He is the patron of Scotland, Greece and Russia.</p>`,
  },
  'sao-nicolau': {
    nome: `Saint Nicholas`,
    resumo: `A Greek bishop famous for his secret generosity, the historical origin of Santa Claus.`,
    texto: `<p>Bishop of Myra, in present-day Turkey, Nicholas became famous for his quiet generosity — the best-known tradition tells that he secretly threw gold coins through the window of a poor family to save three sisters from a cruel fate, without wanting to be recognized. This reputation as a secret giver and protector of children gave rise, over the centuries, to the figure of Santa Claus.</p>`,
  },
  'juan-diego': {
    nome: `Saint Juan Diego`,
    resumo: `The Mexican Indigenous man to whom Our Lady of Guadalupe appeared in 1531.`,
    texto: `<p>An Indigenous man converted to Christianity in colonial Mexico, Juan Diego was the one who received the apparitions of Our Lady of Guadalupe on Tepeyac hill, and was sent by her to ask the bishop to build a shrine. As a sign, his tilma (cloak) was imprinted with the image of Mary, still venerated today by millions of pilgrims. He was the first Indigenous person of the Americas to be canonized.</p>`,
  },
  'santa-luzia': {
    nome: `Saint Lucy`,
    resumo: `A young Sicilian martyr, invoked as protector of eyesight.`,
    texto: `<p>A young woman consecrated to God in Sicily, Lucy gave her dowry to the poor instead of marrying, which led her rejected suitor to denounce her as a Christian. Her name, linked to the Latin word for "light", led popular tradition to associate her with the protection of the eyes and of sight, which is why she is one of the saints most invoked by those who suffer from eye problems.</p>`,
  },
  'pedro-canisio': {
    nome: `Saint Peter Canisius`,
    resumo: `A Dutch Jesuit who wrote catechisms that were decisive in the Catholic Reformation.`,
    texto: `<p>One of the first Jesuits, Peter Canisius devoted his life to strengthening the Catholic faith in regions of Europe deeply shaken by the Protestant Reformation, above all in Germany and Austria. His catechisms, written in clear and accessible language, were reprinted hundreds of times and used to form generations of Catholics. He is a Doctor of the Church.</p>`,
  },
  'estevao-protomartir': {
    nome: `Saint Stephen, Protomartyr`,
    resumo: `The first Christian martyr, stoned while forgiving his executioners.`,
    texto: `<p>One of the first deacons chosen by the Christian community of Jerusalem to care for the poor, Stephen was accused of blasphemy for his speeches about Christ and stoned to death by an enraged crowd. He died forgiving his executioners and praying for them, in front of a young Pharisee named Saul who was guarding the cloaks of those stoning him — the same Saul who would later become Saint Paul.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-evangelista': {
    nome: `Saint John the Evangelist`,
    resumo: `The "beloved disciple", the only apostle who was not martyred, author of the fourth Gospel.`,
    texto: `<p>Called "the disciple whom Jesus loved", John was the only apostle who stayed at the foot of the cross, receiving from the dying Jesus the care of his own mother. The author of the fourth Gospel, three letters and the Book of Revelation, he is the only one of the twelve apostles who was not martyred, living to an advanced age in Ephesus.</p>`,
  },
  'tomas-becket': {
    nome: `Saint Thomas Becket`,
    resumo: `Archbishop of Canterbury, murdered inside his own cathedral for defending the Church before the king.`,
    texto: `<p>A close friend and chancellor of King Henry II of England, Thomas Becket surprised everyone by becoming, as archbishop of Canterbury, a firm defender of the independence of the Church from royal power — which broke his friendship with the king. He was murdered by knights inside his own cathedral, becoming one of the strongest symbols of the freedom of the Church before the State.</p>`,
  },
});

// Lote 8 (setembro de 2026): os 27 santos que vieram do aplicativo
Object.assign(window.BIOGRAFIAS_TRADUZIDAS.en, {
  'charbel-makhlouf': {
    nome: `Saint Charbel Makhlouf`,
    resumo: `The hermit monk of Lebanon, known for his silence, his prayer and the healings at his tomb.`,
    texto: `<p>Youssef Antoun Makhlouf was born in Lebanon in 1828. As a young man he entered the Maronite monastery of Saint Maron in Annaya and received the name Charbel. After many years of community life, in 1875 he was allowed to live as a hermit in a small hermitage next to the monastery.</p>
<p>He spent the rest of his life in prayer, silence, work and penance, with a great love for the Eucharist. He died in 1898, and ever since many of the faithful have reported graces and healings at his tomb. He was canonized by Paul VI in 1977 and is venerated by Christians throughout the East and the whole world.</p>`,
  },
  'gianna-molla': {
    nome: `Saint Gianna Beretta Molla`,
    resumo: `The Italian doctor and mother who gave her life so that her daughter could be born.`,
    texto: `<p>Gianna Beretta was born in Italy in 1922 and graduated in Medicine and Surgery, specializing in pediatrics. She married Pietro Molla, had children and kept practicing her profession with great tenderness toward her patients, especially mothers and children.</p>
<p>During her fourth pregnancy she discovered a tumor in her uterus and, fully aware of the risks, asked that her baby's life be protected. In April 1962 her daughter Gianna Emanuela was born, and the mother died a week later. She was canonized by John Paul II in 2004.</p>`,
  },
  'damiao-molokai': {
    nome: `Saint Damien of Molokai`,
    resumo: `The Belgian priest who served people with leprosy on the Hawaiian island of Molokai.`,
    texto: `<p>Jozef De Veuster was born in Belgium in 1840 and entered the Congregation of the Sacred Hearts, taking the name Damien. He was sent as a missionary to Hawaii and, in 1873, volunteered to serve the people with leprosy who lived in isolation on the Kalaupapa peninsula, on the island of Molokai.</p>
<p>There he cared for the sick, built houses and a church, organized the community and celebrated the sacraments. Years later he caught the disease himself and died in 1889. He was canonized by Benedict XVI in 2009 and is remembered as an example of fearless charity.</p>`,
  },
  'paulo-vi': {
    nome: `Saint Paul VI`,
    resumo: `The pope who guided the Church during and after the Second Vatican Council.`,
    texto: `<p>Giovanni Battista Montini was born in Italy in 1897 and ordained a priest in 1920. He worked in the Vatican Secretariat of State, became archbishop of Milan and, in 1963, was elected pope, taking the name Paul VI.</p>
<p>He carried on the Second Vatican Council, which closed in 1965, and made historic journeys, such as those to the Holy Land and to the headquarters of the United Nations. He governed the Church until his death in 1978. He was canonized by Pope Francis in 2018.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-xxiii': {
    nome: `Saint John XXIII`,
    resumo: `The "Good Pope", who convened the Second Vatican Council.`,
    texto: `<p>Angelo Giuseppe Roncalli was born in 1881 into a peasant family in northern Italy. He was a priest, a diplomat in the service of the Holy See and patriarch of Venice, until he was elected pope in 1958, taking the name John XXIII.</p>
<p>Known for his simplicity and kindness, he convened the Second Vatican Council, which opened in 1962. He died in 1963, before the Council ended. He was canonized in 2014, together with John Paul II, by Pope Francis.</p>`,
  },
  'oscar-romero': {
    nome: `Saint Óscar Romero`,
    resumo: `The archbishop of San Salvador who defended the poor and was killed while celebrating Mass.`,
    texto: `<p>Óscar Arnulfo Romero was born in El Salvador in 1917. He became a priest, then a bishop, and in 1977 archbishop of San Salvador. Faced with the violence striking the people of his country, he began to denounce injustice and defend the dignity of the poorest.</p>
<p>On March 24, 1980, he was murdered while celebrating Mass in a hospital chapel. He was beatified in 2015 and canonized by Pope Francis in 2018.</p>`,
  },
  'josemaria-escriva': {
    nome: `Saint Josemaría Escrivá`,
    resumo: `The Spanish priest who taught that everyday work can be a path to holiness.`,
    texto: `<p>Josemaría Escrivá was born in Spain in 1902 and ordained a priest in 1925. On October 2, 1928, he founded Opus Dei, which promotes the pursuit of holiness in work and in the ordinary tasks of life.</p>
<p>He taught that daily life, lived with love and competence, can be offered to God. He died in Rome in 1975 and was canonized by John Paul II in 2002.</p>`,
  },
  'camilo-lelis': {
    nome: `Saint Camillus de Lellis`,
    resumo: `The soldier who was converted and devoted his life to caring for the sick.`,
    texto: `<p>Camillus de Lellis was born in Italy in 1550 and, as a young man, was a soldier and a gambler. After a deep conversion he began working in a hospital in Rome, where he saw how often the sick were mistreated.</p>
<p>Once ordained a priest, he founded the Order of the Ministers of the Sick, the Camillians, devoted to caring for the sick, even in times of plague. He died in 1614 and is the patron saint of the sick, of hospitals and of health workers.</p>`,
  },
  'roberto-belarmino': {
    nome: `Saint Robert Bellarmine`,
    resumo: `The Jesuit and cardinal, Doctor of the Church, author of a famous catechism.`,
    texto: `<p>Robert Bellarmine was born in Italy in 1542 and entered the Society of Jesus. He was a teacher, writer and theologian, and became a cardinal. His studies of the Catholic faith were very important in a time of great religious debate.</p>
<p>He wrote simple catechisms, used to teach doctrine to children and adults. He died in 1621 and was declared a Doctor of the Church in 1931.</p>`,
  },
  'gemma-galgani': {
    nome: `Saint Gemma Galgani`,
    resumo: `The young mystic of Lucca, known for her prayer and her love for the Passion of Christ.`,
    texto: `<p>Gemma Galgani was born in Lucca, Italy, in 1878. She was orphaned young and went through many hardships of health and money, which she lived with faith and patience.</p>
<p>She was known for her intense prayer and her devotion to the Passion of Christ. She died in 1903, at the age of 25, and was canonized in 1940.</p>`,
  },
  'gerardo-majela': {
    nome: `Saint Gerard Majella`,
    resumo: `The Redemptorist brother, patron saint of mothers expecting a baby.`,
    texto: `<p>Gerard Majella was born in Italy in 1726 into a humble family and worked from a young age as a tailor's apprentice. He joined the Redemptorists as a lay brother, doing simple chores and caring for those in need.</p>
<p>He died in 1755, at the age of 29. He was canonized in 1904 and is invoked as the patron saint of expectant mothers and of mothers.</p>`,
  },
  'leopoldo-mandic': {
    nome: `Saint Leopold Mandić`,
    resumo: `The little Capuchin friar who spent hours in the confessional.`,
    texto: `<p>Bogdan Ivan Mandić was born in 1866 into a Croatian family in Herceg Novi, on the Dalmatian coast (today Montenegro), and entered the Order of Friars Minor Capuchin, taking the name Leopold. He was short and in frail health, but deeply kind in the way he treated people.</p>
<p>He lived for many years in Padua, Italy, where he heard confessions for long hours every day. He died in 1942 and was canonized in 1983 by John Paul II.</p>`,
  },
  'maria-mazzarello': {
    nome: `Saint Mary Dominic Mazzarello`,
    resumo: `The co-foundress of the Salesian Sisters, devoted to the education of girls.`,
    texto: `<p>Mary Dominic Mazzarello was born in Italy in 1837 into a family of farmers. While still young, she devoted herself to teaching the girls of her village, with practical skills and catechism.</p>
<p>With the support of Saint John Bosco, she founded the Institute of the Daughters of Mary Help of Christians, the Salesian Sisters, dedicated to educating young people. She died in 1881 and was canonized in 1951.</p>`,
  },
  'luis-orione': {
    nome: `Saint Louis Orione`,
    resumo: `The Italian priest of the poor, founder of the Little Work of Divine Providence.`,
    texto: `<p>Louis Orione was born in Italy in 1872 and met Saint John Bosco as a boy. After being ordained a priest, he founded the Little Work of Divine Providence, which cares for the poor, orphans, the sick and the elderly.</p>
<p>He sent missionaries to several countries, including Brazil. He died in 1940 and was canonized in 2004.</p>`,
  },
  'edwiges': {
    nome: `Saint Hedwig`,
    resumo: `The duchess of Silesia who helped the poor and is invoked by people in debt.`,
    texto: `<p>Hedwig was born in the 12th century and married Duke Henry I of Silesia. Even as a duchess she lived simply, founded a monastery and helped many poor and sick people.</p>
<p>After her husband's death she withdrew to the monastery, where she lived until she died in 1243. She was canonized in 1267 and, in Brazil, is often invoked by people facing financial hardship.</p>`,
  },
  'zita': {
    nome: `Saint Zita`,
    resumo: `The housemaid of Lucca, patron saint of those who work in domestic service.`,
    texto: `<p>Zita was born in Italy in the 13th century into a poor family and began working as a housemaid while still a girl. For decades she served in the same house, doing each day's work with care and prayer.</p>
<p>She was generous with the poor and patient with those who treated her badly. She died in 1272 and is the patron saint of domestic workers.</p>`,
  },
  'teresa-dos-andes': {
    nome: `Saint Teresa of the Andes`,
    resumo: `The young Chilean Carmelite, the first saint of Chile.`,
    texto: `<p>Juanita Fernández Solar was born in Santiago, Chile, in 1900. From childhood she was cheerful, loved sports and horseback riding, and had a deep life of prayer.</p>
<p>At 19 she entered the Carmel of Los Andes, receiving the name Teresa of Jesus. She died in 1920, less than a year later, and was canonized by John Paul II in 1993, the first Chilean saint.</p>`,
  },
  'pedro-eymard': {
    nome: `Saint Peter Julian Eymard`,
    resumo: `The French priest known as the apostle of the Eucharist.`,
    texto: `<p>Peter Julian Eymard was born in France in 1811 and ordained a priest in 1834. After some years in the Society of Mary, he felt called to spread adoration of the Blessed Sacrament.</p>
<p>He founded the Congregation of the Blessed Sacrament and promoted Eucharistic adoration. He died in 1868 and was canonized in 1962.</p>`,
  },
  'nha-chica': {
    nome: `Blessed Nhá Chica`,
    resumo: `The humble woman of Baependi (Minas Gerais), counselor of the poor and the first Black laywoman beatified in Brazil.`,
    texto: `<p>Francisca de Paula de Jesus, known as Nhá Chica, was born around 1808 in Minas Gerais. She never went to school, but people sought her out for her wisdom, her advice and her prayer.</p>
<p>She used the little she had to help the poor and built a chapel in Baependi. She died in 1895 and was beatified in 2013.</p>`,
  },
  'albertina-berkenbrock': {
    nome: `Blessed Albertina Berkenbrock`,
    resumo: `The 12-year-old girl from Santa Catarina, beatified as a martyr of purity.`,
    texto: `<p>Albertina Berkenbrock was born in Santa Catarina, Brazil, in 1919, into a farming family of German descent. She was a devout girl who helped her family and often went to church.</p>
<p>She was murdered in 1931, at the age of 12, while defending her dignity. She was beatified in 2007, the first Blessed from Santa Catarina.</p>`,
  },
  'catarina-laboure': {
    nome: `Saint Catherine Labouré`,
    resumo: `The Vincentian sister to whom the Miraculous Medal was entrusted.`,
    texto: `<p>Catherine Labouré was born in France in 1806 into a farming family. She entered the Company of the Daughters of Charity in Paris, where she lived simply, caring for the elderly.</p>
<p>In 1830 she reported apparitions of Our Lady, who asked her to have the Miraculous Medal struck. She died in 1876 and was canonized in 1947.</p>`,
  },
  'isabel-portugal': {
    nome: `Saint Elizabeth of Portugal`,
    resumo: `The Portuguese queen known as a peacemaker.`,
    texto: `<p>Elizabeth of Aragon was born in 1271 and married King Denis of Portugal. As queen she helped the poor, founded hospitals and works of charity, and worked for peace between relatives in conflict.</p>
<p>After she was widowed, she lived beside the convent of Saint Clare in Coimbra. She died in 1336 and was canonized in 1625.</p>`,
  },
  'nuno-alvares': {
    nome: `Saint Nuno of Saint Mary`,
    resumo: `The Constable of Portugal who ended his life as a Carmelite friar.`,
    texto: `<p>Nuno Álvares Pereira was born in 1360 and stood out as a military leader, defending the independence of Portugal with victories such as Aljubarrota, in 1385.</p>
<p>Having fulfilled his duties, he gave away his possessions and entered the Carmo Convent in Lisbon as Brother Nuno of Saint Mary. He died in 1431 and was canonized in 2009.</p>`,
  },
  'joao-de-britto': {
    nome: `Saint John de Britto`,
    resumo: `The Portuguese Jesuit martyred in India.`,
    texto: `<p>John de Britto was born in Lisbon in 1647 and raised at the royal court. He entered the Society of Jesus and left as a missionary for India.</p>
<p>He adapted to the local culture, living simply. He was killed in 1693 and canonized in 1947.</p>`,
  },
  'charles-foucauld': {
    nome: `Saint Charles de Foucauld`,
    resumo: `The hermit of the Sahara who sought to live the Gospel in silence and friendship.`,
    texto: `<p>Charles de Foucauld was born in France in 1858. After a youth marked by military life, he underwent a deep conversion and lived as a monk, and later as a priest, in the Algerian Sahara.</p>
<p>There he lived among the Tuareg, with humility, friendship and prayer. He was killed in 1916 and canonized by Pope Francis in 2022.</p>`,
  },
  'grignion-montfort': {
    nome: `Saint Louis-Marie Grignion de Montfort`,
    resumo: `The French missionary who taught true devotion to Mary.`,
    texto: `<p>Louis-Marie Grignion de Montfort was born in France in 1673 and ordained a priest in 1700. He preached popular missions in many regions of the country, always with great love for Our Lady.</p>
<p>He wrote the "Treatise on True Devotion to the Blessed Virgin". He died in 1716 and was canonized in 1947.</p>`,
  },
  'tarcisio': {
    nome: `Saint Tarcisius`,
    resumo: `The Roman boy who protected the Eucharist, patron saint of altar servers.`,
    texto: `<p>According to tradition, Tarcisius was a young Christian who lived in Rome in the first centuries of the Church, in the time of the persecutions. One day he was entrusted with carrying the Eucharist to imprisoned Christians.</p>
<p>On the way he was attacked and died protecting the Blessed Sacrament. He is venerated as the patron saint of altar servers and of extraordinary ministers of Holy Communion.</p>`,
  },
});
